ng demand is met by means of the return policy, or it is lost. However, if the ERC has ordered the largest amount of demanded supplies from the supplier, then the hospital would not order the maximum amount of supplies from the supplier. Since the highest stock level in the ERC means that the ERC has unused emergency medicines after an emergency response, then there must be expired items in the hospital at the end of the period if the hospital orders the maximum amount; thus, it is better for the hospital to seek an equilibrium order amount in all circumstances. 5. Centralized decisions To find the best possible result in this closed-loop supply chain, we develop a decision setting by which the ERC and the hospital are centralized. In the centralized setting, the ERC would not charge the hospital for returns, it would take on the costs of transferring inventory. This centralized setting can be accomplished through some practical contracts, and this problem should be researched in greater depth in future work. Combining Eqs ( 7 ) and ( 8 ), we get the following expression: C c = ∫ a θ T ∫ I d s 1 ( x − I ) f ( x ) g ( t ) d x d t − ∫ a θ T ∫ c I v t ( I − x ) f ( x ) g ( t ) d x d t − ∫ θ T b v θ T I g ( t ) d t + ∫ a θ T ∫ c I r ( I − x ) f ( x ) g ( t ) d x d t + ∫ θ T b r I g ( t ) d t + h 1 I T ∫ a θ T ∫ I d s 2 ( y T − Q ) f ( x ) g ( t ) d x d t + ∫ a θ T ∫ I d h 2 Q 2 Q y f ( x ) g ( t ) d x d t + ∫ a θ T ∫ c I v t ( I − x ) f ( x ) g ( t ) d x d t + ∫ a θ T ∫ I + Q − y T I s 2 ( y T − ( Q + I − x ) ) f ( x ) g ( t ) d x d t + ∫ a θ T ∫ c I + Q − y T e ( Q + I − x − y T ) f ( x ) g ( t ) d x d t + ∫ θ T b e ( I + Q − y T ) g ( t ) d t + ∫ a θ T ∫ I + Q − y T I h 2 ( ( Q + Q − y t ) t 2 + ( Q − y t + I − x ) 2 2 y ) f ( x ) g ( t ) d x d t + ∫ a θ T ∫ c I + Q − y T h 2 ( ( Q + I − x ) T − y T 2 T − ( I − x ) t ) f ( x ) g ( t ) d x d t + ∫ θ T b h 2 ( ( I + Q ) T − y T 2 2 − I t ) g ( t ) d t + ∫ θ T b v θ T I g ( t ) d t (18) ∂ C c ∂ I = − s 1 ( 1 − F ( I ) ) 