INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5237,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12177439','Laryngotracheal Pathologies and Symptoms Associated to Airway Management of Critically Ill COVID-19 Patients at One-Year Follow Up: An Observational Study.','Clara SL, Agneta K, Elin M, Jessica K, Mathias VB, Johan M, Michael W, Andrea V, Boel H, Amalia AL, Louise S, Jeremy W.','2025','laryngological tracheotomized tracheotomized laryngotracheoscopy vhi vhi vhi tracheomalacia tracheostoma sequels laryngotracheoscopy admittance u071 u072 gba00 gbb00 gbb03 gbb96 ailos anaesthetized sprayed naphazoline photographed enf fiberscope processor photographed powershot g7x filmed adduction laryngologist dysmotility ankylosis cricoarytenoid fiberscope proximally distally laryngologist tethered pretracheal vhi clopper halum retracheotomy v30 tracheotomies tracheotomies tracheotomies tracheotomized tracheotomized saps3 pao2 fio2 saps3 unattainable dysmotility lambdoid tracheotomized halum clopper laryngotracheoscopy dysmotility retracheotomy vhi vhi reintubations reintubations retracheotomy retracheotomy retracheotomy tracheotomized retracheotomy retracheotomy retracheotomy laryngological rouhani perroni laryngotracheoscopy vhi vhi vhi vhi vhi vhi napping halum fiacchini tracheoesophageal mangiameli cranially tracheotomized vhi sverige laryngologica agneta elin anaesthesiologica e70074 stiftelsen laryngologica piazza filauro dikkers laryngological fiacchini trico tavares tiago rouhani clunie thong e1918 e1925 naunheim puka bourdillon panth ihnat kohli laryngological banda guenthart mangiameli perroni costantino perroni radovanovic mondoni bouldin sandeep tkaczuk brattstrom agvald ohman anaesthesiologica praveen safa ostberg laryngologica ntouniadakis brus beckerath ohlsson dotevall logopedics phoniatrics vocology halum plowman hilel karatayli ozgursoy benke postextubation norwood vallina saigusa mclarty yiu yildiz desilva matrka posttracheostomy fiacchini trico ribechini fiacchini proietti poma','ABSTRACT Background The European laryngological society predicted an increased incidence of laryngotracheal complications as a result of the COVID‐19 pandemic. During the first pandemic wave in the Stockholm region, 31% of critically ill COVID‐19 patients were tracheotomized by an open surgical (OST) or a percutaneous tracheotomy (PCT). The aim of this study was to investigate the incidence of visible laryngotracheal pathologies in tracheotomized and long‐term intubated COVID‐19 survivors ≥ 12 months after initial intubation, to examine whether these pathologies were symptomatic and to assess possible associated factors. Methods Study participants underwent laryngotracheoscopy under local anaesthesia, and tracheostomy skin and soft tissue scars were photo documented. Patient‐reported outcome measures — the Voice Handicap Index‐10 (VHI‐10), the Eating Assessment Tool‐10 (EAT‐10) and the Dyspnea Index (DI), and demographics were retrospectively extracted from patient medical records. Results Of 73 included study participants (40 OST, 24 PCT and 9 long‐term intubated), 58% had visible laryngotracheal pathologies. Tracheostomy tube size and the number of days with tracheostomy were associated with skin and soft tissue pathology and tracheal pathology ( p < 0.05). The results of the VHI‐10 and EAT‐10 were congruent with both laryngeal and skin and soft tissue pathologies. Participants with the highest DI scores, indicating breathing difficulties, had both laryngeal and tracheal pathologies followed by tracheal pathology alone. Conclusions A high incidence of visible laryngotracheal pathologies, two airway management‐related factors, and symptom‐pathology associations for VHI‐10 and EAT‐10 scores were found in a cohort of COVID‐19 survivors ≥ 12 months after critical care. 1. Introduction Early in the COVID‐19 pandemic, the European laryngeal society predicted an increased workload for otolaryngologists. Emergency airway management, usage of large size endotracheal tubes (ETT) and tracheostomy tubes (TT) and the necessity of prone body position were all expected to increase the risk for upper airway pathology [ 1 ]. Initial reports after COVID‐19 critical care indeed reported a higher‐than‐expected incidence of laryngotracheal complications affecting patient voice, swallowing, and breathing [ 2 , 3 , 4 ]. However, similar symptoms were reported in COVID‐19 patients not subjected to intensive care [ 5 , 6 ]. In COVID‐19 survivors, dysphagia has been reported to be associated with intubation, tracheostomy, or prone position [ 7 , 8 ], and dysphonia has been related to reintubation and duration of intubation [ 9 ], visible pathology was not assessed in these studies. An increased incidence of subglottic stenosis [ 10 ], stenosis of the trachea, or tracheomalacia at the site of the previous tracheostoma [ 11 , 12 ] has been reported among COVID‐19 survivors. This increased incidence is in line with emerging reports of post‐COVID‐19 sequels where dyspnea and cough are highlighted as the most frequent and enduring of the respiratory symptoms, their most severe manifestations correlated to hospitalization, with some even increasing over time [ 13 ]. The aim of this study was to investigate the incidence of visible laryngotracheal pathologies through laryngotracheoscopy of COVID‐19 survivors 1 year after admittance to an intensive care unit (ICU) in Stockholm, Sweden. Secondarily, we wanted to study whether these pathologies were directly symptomatic and assess possible associated factors. 2. Methods 2.1. Study Participants Patients admitted to an ICU with a diagnosis of COVID‐19 at any of the Stockholm region''s eight emergency hospitals (February 15, 2020 to August 31, 2020) were identified through local hospital database systems. Patients with ICD‐10 codes for COVID‐19 (U071 or U072) and tracheotomy (GBA00, GBB00, GBB03 or GBB96) were identified, and survivors were extracted. Patients intubated ≥ 14 days were identified from hospital ICU databases in those hospitals where ENT staff and therefore tracheostomy were not readily available (3/8 hospitals) [ 14 ]. Adults (≥ 18 years) who were non‐Swedish speaking or unable to give informed consent were excluded. Eligible participants were contacted ≥ 1 year after the initial intubation date. Demographic and clinical data was extracted from patient medical records 1 year after ICU care. Participants were invited to our research clinic where they underwent a clinical examination by one of two otorhinolaryngologists. 2.2. Clinical Examination Participants inhaled Lidocaine hydrochloride Apl (40 mg/mL) using an Ailos inhalation system driven by air over a 3‐min period. The nasal passage was anaesthetized, sprayed, by a mixture of Naphazoline (0.17 mg/mL) and Lidocaine (34 mg/mL). The larynx and trachea were then examined (upright patient position) and photographed with an Olympus ENF‐V3 flexible fiberscope and Olympus CV‐170 light source and processor. The tracheostomy skin and soft tissue scar in the front of the neck was photographed (Canon PowerShot G7x) and filmed during a swallowing maneuver. 2.3. Determination of Pathology Laryngeal pathology was defined as posterior glottis edema or fibrosis, vocal cord immobility (including asymmetric or reduced abduction/adduction movements) and granulation tissue. An independent laryngologist confirmed findings of laryngotracheal pathology from the first clinical examination (photos and clinical notes), and in most cases, patients were examined a second time, with video laryngoscopy, to further verify the diagnosis. In cases with vocal fold immobility/dysmotility, further investigation with laryngeal electromyography, to differentiate between damage of the recurrent laryngeal nerve or ankylosis of the cricoarytenoid joint, was not included in the study protocol. Tracheal pathology was defined as a narrowed tracheal lumen due to scar formation. The pathology was photo documented at three positions with the flexible fiberscope: from the subglottic level and from the levels just proximally and distally of the tracheal pathology. Narrowing was confirmed by an independent laryngologist. As patients were not under general anesthesia, an exact measurement of the tracheal lumen at the pathology site or palpation of the stenosis was not performed. A visual narrowing of ≥ 50% of the tracheal lumen was defined as clinically significant as defined by James [ 15 ]. Pathological skin and soft tissue scar was defined as adherent (tethered) skin and soft tissue to the pretracheal area [ 16 ] which can cause inversion and co‐movement with the larynx and trachea, interfering with the swallowing process. 2.4. Patient Reported Outcome Measures (PROMs) Three validated study questionnaires were used to assess subjective symptoms at the time of the examination; the Eating Assessment Tool (EAT‐10, 0–40 points, > 3 indicates pathology) [ 17 ], the (DI, 0–40 points) [ 18 ] and the Voice Handicap Index‐10 (VHI‐10, 0–40 points, > 6 points indicate a difference of importance) [ 19 ]. 2.5. Ethical Approval and Considerations The study was approved by the Swedish Ethical Review Authority (2020–02779). 2.6. Statistical Analysis Numerical variables are described with mean and standard deviation (SD), and categorical variables are described with percentage and range. Incidence of pathology in the population is reported, and an uncertainty quantification is provided via the exact Clopper‐Pearson 95% confidence interval. The observed incidence rate was also compared to the incidence rate reported in Halum et al. [ 20 ]. Univariable logistic regression models were fitted to evaluate each risk factor. Risk factors with statistically significant univariable effects were further analyzed in a multivariable logistic regression model which included controls for body mass index (BMI), reintubation, days intubated, tracheostomy, TT size, retracheotomy, and days with tracheostomy when clinically indicated [ 20 ]. Statistical analysis was performed using IBM SPSS software v30. 3. Results During the first 6 months of the pandemic, 904 COVID‐19 patients required mechanical ventilation in the Stockholm region. A total of 281 tracheotomies were performed, 216 open surgical tracheotomies (OST) and 65 percutaneous tracheotomies (PCT). OST was almost exclusively performed by otorhinolaryngologists and PCT by anesthesiologists. Of the 281 tracheotomized patients, 119 were eligible for the study (83 were deceased and 79 were initially excluded due to non‐Swedish speaking, inability to consent, resident of another health care region or low age). A flow chart of inclusion is shown in Figure 1 . 64/119 accepted study participation, 40 OST and 24 PCT. Details of the 55/119 excluded tracheotomized participants are listed in Table S1 . Of the 25 surviving participants who were intubated ≥ 14 days (no tracheotomy) and eligible for the study, nine accepted study participation. Details of the 16/25 excluded long‐term intubated participants are listed in Table S1 . Two of the study participants did not consent to be examined as they previously had been examined by an otolaryngologist. In five participants, the ETT size during critical care was not noted in the medical journal. FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Flow chart: Inclusion of study participants. Demographic data grouped by airway management technique is presented in Table 1 . The three groups were comparable regarding medical background except for diabetes mellitus, which was more prevalent in the two tracheostomy groups. Tracheostomy was associated with a shorter duration of endotracheal intubation, as well as an overall longer stay in the ICU. A comparison between the groups of included and excluded study participants (Table S1 ) confirms similar demographics. TABLE 1. Demographics of study population. Subjects included ( n = 73) Intubated only Open surgical tracheotomy Percutaneous tracheotomy ( n = 9) ( n = 40) ( n = 24) Gender (m/f) 8/1 33/7 21/3 Age at intubation or tracheotomy (mean years, range) 55.1 (43–69) 53.3 (28–69) 60.4 (43–75) BMI (sd) 31.6 (6.1) 31.4 (6.7) 28.9 (4.7) Smoker (%) 0 2.5 0 Previous smoker (%) 44.4 40 45.83 Non‐smoker (%) 55.6 57.5 54.17 Asthma (%) 22.2 15 12.5 Other lung disease (%) 11.1 2.5 0 Hypertension (%) 22.2 35 58.3 Diabetes mellitus (%) 0 22.5 33.3 SAPS3 (sd) 51.1 (6.7) 51.7 (7.2) 58.9 (8.8) Minimum PFI day of admission (sd) 12 (8.6) * 12.3 (5.7) * 11.4 (2.8) Days in ICU (range) 19.8 (16–29) 37.7 (17–78) 28.3 (8–59) Required ECMO (total patients) 0 7 0 Time at follow‐up (years) 1.1 1.0 1.0 Days with tracheostomy (range) — 23.3 (5–70) 14.3 (6–30) Days intubated (range) 16 (14–20) 13 (3–25) 10.5 (4–32) Prone position, ETT (%) 100 77.5 83.3 Prone position, TT (%) — 5 4.2 Open in a new tab Note: Time at follow‐up was defined as years from initial intubation at the ICU to the day of follow‐up. Abbreviations: BMI, body mass index; ECMO, Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation; f, female; ICU, intensive care unit; m, male; PFI, PaO2 kPa/FiO2; SAPS3, simplified acute physiology score; sd, standard deviation. * Data was unattainable in more than 10% of the patient group. Laryngotracheal pathology was identified in 42/73 (58%) of the study participants, eight with laryngeal pathology alone, 22 with tracheal pathology alone, and 12 with pathologies at both sites (Table 2 ). The laryngeal pathologies ( n = 20) consisted of vocal cord dysmotility/immobility in 15 cases, laryngeal oedema in four cases, and one case with laryngeal granuloma. The tracheal pathologies ( n = 34) were all grades of lambdoid or A‐frame shaped stenosis in the area of the tracheotomy incision (Figure S1 ). 11 (32%) of the cases with tracheal stenosis were defined as a significant reduction of the tracheal lumen (> 50% visually), of which all cases were in the OST group (Figure 2A and Figure S1 ). Only one of nine long‐term intubated participants presented with laryngeal pathology (laryngeal granuloma), and none had visible tracheal pathologies. Tracheotomized study participants with visible tracheal pathology was present in 44.4% (CI 31.9%–57.5%) of individuals in our study, which is significantly higher than the reported incidence of 1.7% seen in Halum et al. [ 20 ] ( p < 0.001, exact binomial test and corresponding exact Clopper‐Pearson 95% CI). TABLE 2. Pathology detected with laryngotracheoscopy 1 year after ICU care. Laryngeal pathology Tracheal pathology (stenosis) Soft tissue scarring Vocal cord dysmotility immobility Oedema Granuloma Total Non‐significant stenosis Significant stenosis a Total Intubated ( n = 9) 0 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 OST ( n = 40) 14 2 1 17 16 11 27 18 PCT ( n = 24) 1 1 0 2 7 0 7 5 Open in a new tab a Significant stenosis is defined as a visual narrowing of the trachea of more than 50%. FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab Factors associated with laryngotracheal and skin and soft tissue pathology. (A) percentage of participants with tracheal or laryngeal pathology. (B) days with tracheostomy. (C) percentage of participants requiring retracheotomy. (D) TT size. Mean with standard deviation is presented. Dyspnea according to DI was associated with tracheal pathology alone (OR 1.10 (95% CI 1.03–1.16); p = 0.002) but not with laryngeal pathology alone (OR 1.04 (95% CI 0.99–1.10), p = 0.15) (Figure 3A ). Notably, a significant increase of DI score was found when comparing the score results of both laryngeal and tracheal pathologies to “no pathology” (Mann Whitney U p = 0.003) but not for laryngeal or tracheal pathology separately ( p > 0.05). Reported subjective voice symptoms (VHI‐10) were associated with identifiable laryngeal pathology (OR 1.13 (95% CI 1.05–1.22), p = 0.001, Figure 3B ). FIGURE 3. Open in a new tab Reported subjective symptoms in the presence of or without tracheal (A), laryngeal (A, B), or skin and soft tissue (C) pathology. (A) Dyspnoea index (DI) as a measure of dyspnoea, (B) VHI‐10 as a measure of dysphonia, and C, EAT‐10 as a measure of dysphagia. (A, B) Median with upper and lower quartile (box), and mean (X) is presented. (C) Mean with standard deviation is presented. An analysis of possible associated factors for laryngeal pathology revealed that a commonly reported risk factor, reintubation during critical care, was not significant in this study (OR 1.09 (95% CI 0.56–2.10), p = 0.81), a trend towards an increased number of reintubations contributing to a higher percentage of laryngeal pathology was identified no reintubation 10/44 (22.7%), one reintubation 6/18 (33.3%), two reintubations 3/7 (42.9%). A patient history of smoking, diabetes mellitus, treatment with corticosteroids during critical care, body mass index (BMI), ETT size, and number of days intubated showed no association to laryngeal pathology (Table S2 ), this study was not powered to rule out these associations. Regarding tracheal pathology, analysis of associated factors showed that a longer period with tracheostomy (days with tracheostomy) increased the risk significantly (OR 1.12 (95% CI 1.04–1.21), p = 0.002) (Figure 2B ). Retracheotomy was not shown to be a significant risk factor (OR 2.08 (95% CI 0.57–7.63), p = 0.27) (Figure 2C ). Days with tracheostomy was still a significant risk factor for tracheal pathology (OR 1.13 (95% CI 1.04–1.23), p = 0.003) when including BMI, TT size and retracheotomy in a multivariable logistic regression analysis. Risk factors such as BMI, history of smoking, asthma, other lung pathology, diabetes mellitus, ETT size or TT size, days intubated, reintubation, use of prone position or treatment with corticosteroids showed no association with tracheal pathology in our material (Table S2 ). Tracheal pathology was more likely to occur using the OST technique when compared to the PCT technique, (OR 4.5 (95% CI 1.52–13.35), p = 0.007). However, this was no longer significant (OR 3.49 (95% CI 0.94–13.1), p = 0.062) when including BMI, TT size, retracheotomy and days with tracheostomy in a multivariable logistic regression analysis. Additionally to the above described laryngotracheal pathologies, a pathological skin and soft tissue scar was identified in 23 (36%) participants (18/40 OST, 5/24 PCT), where nine later required scar revision due to swallowing dysfunction. An analysis of possible associations to pathological scar formation in tracheotomized participants revealed that a larger TT size, independent of insertion technique, significantly increased the risk (Figure 2D , OR 5.42 (95% CI 1.59–18.43), p = 0.007). Days with tracheostomy was also shown to be associated with a pathological scar (OR 1.04 (95% CI 1.00–1.07), p = 0.045). Associations such as BMI, days in ICU, retracheotomy, ETT size, treatment with corticosteroids, prone position, or previous smoking (Table S2 ) showed no correlation to a pathological scar in this study. TT size remained a significant risk factor for a pathological scar (OR 6.50 (95% CI 1.48–28.55), p = 0.013) when BMI, retracheotomy, and days with tracheostomy were included in a multivariable logistic regression analysis. Days with tracheostomy also remained a significant risk factor for a pathological scar (OR 1.07 (95% CI 1.01–1.13), p = 0.018) when BMI, TT size, and retracheotomy were included in a multivariable logistic regression analysis. A pathological EAT‐10 score was found to be related to a pathological scar (OR 1.17 (95% CI 1.03–1.33), p = 0.018) (Figure 3C ). 4. Discussion In line with the prediction of the European Laryngological Society [ 1 ], a high incidence of laryngotracheal pathology was found in this prospective follow‐up study of a COVID‐19 survivors from the first pandemic wave in the Stockholm region. Previous short‐term follow‐up reports of COVID‐19 survivors as well as recent emerging long‐term studies have all presented higher incidences of upper airway complications [ 2 , 3 , 4 , 6 , 13 ] compared to pre‐COVID‐19 reports. Only a few studies have included patients independent of symptom presentation in an attempt to estimate the true incidence of pathology. Rouhani et al. [ 4 ] performed laryngeal examinations on participants after ICU care and found laryngeal pathology in 18.9% of study participants. However, in this study the trachea was not examined. Other studies, where examination of both the larynx and trachea were performed, have estimated upper airway pathology in 40% of patients [ 3 ] at 3 months and 31% [ 2 ] at 6 months after ICU discharge. Our reported incidence of 58% agrees with previous studies; however, our study included only one third of the total surviving population from the first wave of infection, introducing an uncertainty. Others report an even higher incidence of laryngotracheal pathologies [ 5 , 6 , 9 ], however, these studies demonstrate results from a population that has been referred due to objective symptoms, rather than a cross‐section of all hospitalized COVID‐19 patients, inevitably presenting a bias. Perroni et al. [ 12 ] reported an 8.5% incidence of tracheal stenosis on radiological examination but did not screen all patients with laryngotracheoscopy and therefore would not have identified laryngeal pathology or mild stenosis of the trachea. In this study, subjective reporting of dysphonia (VHI‐10) correlated with visible pathology, even though the average VHI‐10 score (6, 10) in the group with visible pathology did not reach the hallmark for VHI‐10 score abnormality (> 11) as stated by previous authors [ 17 ]. The duration of intubation, reintubation, tracheostomy, and prone positioning of intubated patients have all been associated with post‐COVID‐19 dysphonia using VHI‐10 [ 2 , 5 , 9 ]. However, these studies linked risk factors with subjective symptoms and not visible pathology. Our results showed that 12/20 participants reporting a significant rating of VHI‐10 (> 6) revealed visible laryngeal pathology. However, dysphonia can also be present in patients with tracheal stenosis, especially when involving the subglottic area [ 21 ], and therefore could explain the pathological VHI‐10 scores in patients without visible laryngeal pathology. Swallowing has been shown to be impaired in COVID‐19 patients during the immediate post‐ICU period [ 4 , 5 , 7 ], where this incidence was higher than that of non‐COVID‐19 patients with acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) [ 22 ]. In a meta‐analysis of COVID‐19 patients, dysphagia was related to intubation, tracheostomy, or treatment in a prone position [ 8 ]. Also, dysphagia was shown to decrease over time and was reported as mild, by EAT‐10, at 3 months [ 7 ] and one year [ 13 ]. However, these studies do not reveal a direct cause of this dysphagia other than the previously described links to ARDS itself. In our study, the presence of a pathological (napping) skin and soft tissue scar due to the previous tracheostomy correlated to a higher EAT‐10 score. Since the rating was above the cut‐off score of EAT‐10 (indicating a potentially abnormal swallowing) this result implies a clinical importance. The presence of this adhesive soft tissue scar was found to be associated with the use of a larger sized TT and a longer period with the larger TT. The relationship between subjective upper airway dysfunction and visible laryngotracheal pathology in our study population presented interpretation difficulties. Long‐term evaluations of DI scores in hospitalized COVID‐19 cohorts have been reported to remain high, and even increase, after 1 year [ 13 ]. DI scores have also been shown to decrease amongst COVID‐19 survivors after surgical treatment of laryngotracheal stenosis related to airway management [ 6 ]. Participants in our study generally rated a low DI score and only 9% (7/73) rated a score of > 25 indicating significant pathology [ 18 ]. There was a statistical association between a higher DI score and visible tracheal pathology, but the score was only significantly increased above that of “no pathology” in the presence of laryngeal and tracheal pathology together. Other studies have shown that patients with up to 75% narrowing of the tracheal lumen can remain without respiratory symptoms at rest [ 23 ]. To fully understand the upper airway narrowing post‐tracheotomy, we believe that there is a need for airway assessment during patient exertion. Only then can a possible collapse of the tracheal walls at the level of the tracheotomy scar be detected and properly evaluated. Detection of visible pathology does not provide enough information. Future studies should focus on this. Pre‐COVID, Halum et al. reported that airway stenosis was associated with obesity (BMI > 30) in those intubated with an ETT > 7.5 [ 20 ]. Li et al. [ 24 ] associated tracheal narrowing with obesity as well as a larger size TT. Tracheal pathology was not statistically associated with any of the above factors within the limits of our study; however, a longer period with a TT (days with tracheostomy) was found to be related. This factor is not evaluated in previous studies and therefore should be an area of focus in the future. Fiacchini et al. showed that the incidence of tracheal lesions and tracheoesophageal fistulas was greatly increased in patients admitted due to COVID‐19 [ 25 ] and further suggested that this effect may be the result of the use of prone body position, care staff fatigue, a sensitive epithelium due to steroid use, hypoxic injury, and/or the virus itself [ 26 ], although no direct statistical comparison was performed. Mangiameli et al. [ 11 ] noted that a more cranially placed tracheotomy was linked to tracheal stenosis, a factor not assessed in this study. Medical care during the first wave of the COVID‐19 pandemic in the Stockholm region was exceptional, nearly all critically ill COVID‐19 patients were mechanically ventilated and emergency airway management procedures were common. Hopefully, the high incidence of laryngotracheal pathologies found in our study cohort of first pandemic wave survivors is also an exception. Nevertheless, our study results warrant the need for upper airway examination of tracheotomized or long‐term intubated COVID‐19 survivors who still experience voice, swallowing, or breathing difficulties, in order to present treatment options and avoid future airway hazards. 4.1. Study Limitations This study examined a limited group of the first wave of patients during the COVID‐19 pandemic and therefore we could only assess possible associations that would need to be proven with examination in further studies of larger patient groups. However, the demographics of the excluded population were similar and therefore the study population was seen as representative. In addition, a possible selection bias was identified in this study. The patients who chose to participate may have done so due to symptoms of breathing, voice, or swallowing disorders whereas those without symptoms may have decided not to participate. This is a common bias seen in many post‐COVID studies where an individual may have received several invitations to different studies and would then choose those studies that suited their symptom pattern. Therefore, the exact incidence of pathology may vary from the reported statistic in this study. 4.2. Conclusions We report a high incidence of visible laryngotracheal pathologies in a cohort of COVID‐19 survivors ≥ 12 months after critical care. Regarding possible airway management associated factors, TT size and number of days with tracheostomy were found to associate with visible tracheal and skin and soft tissue pathologies. Laryngeal and skin and soft tissue pathologies, through their effect on voice and swallowing, showed to be detectable by VHI‐10 and EAT‐10. However, the association between dyspnea and visible laryngotracheal pathologies, through DI score results, was not as straightforward to establish in this study population. Author Contributions S.L.C. was involved in conceptualization; data curation; formal analysis; funding acquisition; methodology; resources; writing – original draft; and writing – review and editing. J.W. was involved in the steps mentioned above as well as in supervision. M.E., K.J. and V.B.M. were involved in conceptualization; formal analysis; funding acquisition; methodology; supervision; and writing – review and editing and study design. K.A., M.J., W.M., V.A., H.B., A.L.A. and S.L. were involved in data curation. All authors are responsible for the authenticity of the article. Conflicts of Interest The authors declare no conflicts of interest. Supporting information Data S1. AAS-69-0-s001.docx (480.2KB, docx) Acknowledgements We would like to thank Olympus Sverige AB. for the kind loan of the flexible endoscopes and other examination equipment for this study. We acknowledge the support of Acta Oto‐Laryngologica. Clara S. L., Agneta K., Elin M., et al., “Laryngotracheal Pathologies and Symptoms Associated to Airway Management of Critically Ill COVID‐19 Patients at One‐Year Follow Up: An Observational Study,” Acta Anaesthesiologica Scandinavica 69, no. 6 (2025): e70074, 10.1111/aas.70074. Funding: This work was supported by Stiftelsen Acta Oto‐Laryngologica. This follow‐up study presents outcomes at 1 year following ICU care for a single center cohort of COVID‐19 cases that required tracheostomy. Case factors are presented with associations to outcomes. Data Availability Statement The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request. References 1. Piazza C., Filauro M., Dikkers F. G., et al., “Long‐Term Intubation and High Rate of Tracheostomy in COVID‐19 Patients Might Determine an Unprecedented Increase of Airway Stenoses: A Call to Action From the European Laryngological Society,” European Archives of Oto‐Rhino‐Laryngology 278 (2021): 1–7. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Fiacchini G., Abel J. R., Trico D., et al., “Incidence and Types of Laryngotracheal Sequelae of Prolonged Invasive Ventilation in COVID‐19 Patients,” European Archives of Oto‐Rhino‐Laryngology 279 (2022): 5755–5760. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Felix L., Tavares T. L., Almeida V. P. B., and Tiago R. S. L., “Incidence of Laryngotracheal Lesions After Orotracheal Intubation in Coronavirus Disease Patients,” Laryngoscope 132 (2022): 1075–1081. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Rouhani M. J., Clunie G., Thong G., et al., “A Prospective Study of Voice, Swallow, and Airway Outcomes Following Tracheostomy for COVID‐19,” Laryngoscope 131 (2021): E1918–E1925. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Naunheim M. R., Zhou A. S., Puka E., et al., “Laryngeal Complications of COVID‐19,” Laryngoscope Investig Otolaryngol 5 (2020): 1117–1124. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Shah H. P., Bourdillon A. T., Panth N., Ihnat J., and Kohli N., “Long‐Term Laryngological Sequelae and Patient‐Reported Outcomes After COVID‐19 Infection,” American Journal of Otolaryngology 44 (2023): 103780. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Clayton N. A., Freeman‐Sanderson A., and Walker E., “Dysphagia Prevalence and Outcomes Associated With the Evolution of COVID‐19 and Its Variants in Critically Ill Patients,” Dysphagia 39, no. 1 (2023): 109–118. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Lee C. L., Huang G., Banda K. J., et al., “Prevalence of Oropharyngeal Dysphagia and Risk of Mortality Among Hospitalized COVID‐19 Patients: A Meta‐Analysis,” Journal of Global Health 12 (2022): 5058. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Clayton N. A., Freeman‐Sanderson A., and Walker E., “Prevalence and Recovery of Dysphonia in COVID‐19 Patients Requiring Intensive Care Treatment,” Australian Critical Care 37, no. 1 (2023): 151–157. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Krishnan A., Guenthart B. A., Choi A., et al., “Tracheal Stenosis and Airway Complications in the Coronavirus Disease‐19 Era,” Ann Thorac Surg Short Rep 1 (2023): 460–464. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Mangiameli G., Perroni G., Costantino A., et al., “Analysis of Risk Factors for Tracheal Stenosis Managed During COVID‐19 Pandemic: A Retrospective, Case‐Control Study From Two European Referral Centre,” Journal of Personalized Medicine 13, no. 5 (2023): 729. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Perroni G., Radovanovic D., Mondoni M., et al., “Incidence of Tracheal Stenosis in ICU Hospitalized COVID‐19 Patients: Results From a Prospective, Observational, Multicenter Study,” Journal of Personalized Medicine 14, no. 1 (2023): 39. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Bouldin E., Sandeep S., Gillespie A., and Tkaczuk A., “Otolaryngologic Symptom Severity Post SARS‐CoV‐2 Infection,” Journal of Voice (2023). [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Larsson E., Brattstrom O., Agvald‐Ohman C., et al., “Characteristics and Outcomes of Patients With COVID‐19 Admitted to ICU in a Tertiary Hospital in Stockholm, Sweden,” Acta Anaesthesiologica Scandinavica 65 (2021): 76–81. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. James P., Parmar S., Hussain K., and Praveen P., “Tracheal Stenosis After Tracheostomy,” British Journal of Oral & Maxillofacial Surgery 59 (2021): 82–85. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Grant N. and Davison S. P., “Management of the Post‐Tracheostomy Scar,” Laryngoscope 117 (2007): 2107–2109. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Moller R., Safa S., and Ostberg P., “Validation of the Swedish Translation of Eating Assessment Tool (S‐EAT‐10),” Acta Oto‐Laryngologica 136 (2016): 749–753. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Ntouniadakis E., Brus O., and von Beckerath M., “Dyspnea Index: An Upper Airway Obstruction Instrument; Translation and Validation in Swedish,” Clinical Otolaryngology 46 (2021): 380–387. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Ohlsson A. C. and Dotevall H., “Voice Handicap Index in Swedish,” Logopedics, Phoniatrics, Vocology 34 (2009): 60–66. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Halum S. L., Ting J. Y., Plowman E. K., et al., “A Multi‐Institutional Analysis of Tracheotomy Complications,” Laryngoscope 122 (2012): 38–45. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Hilel A. T., Karatayli‐Ozgursoy S., Benke J. R., et al., “Voice Quality in Laryngotracheal Stenosis: Impact of Dilation and Level of Stenosis,” Annals of Otology, Rhinology, and Laryngology 124 (2015): 413–418. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Lin C. W., Chiang T. Y., Chen W. C., et al., “Is Postextubation Dysphagia Underestimated in the Era of COVID‐19? A Systematic Review and Meta‐Analysis,” Otolaryngology and Head and Neck Surgery 168 (2023): 935–943. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Norwood S., Vallina V. L., Short K., Saigusa M., Fernandez L. G., and McLarty J. W., “Incidence of Tracheal Stenosis and Other Late Complications After Percutaneous Tracheostomy,” Annals of Surgery 232 (2000): 233–241. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Li M., Yiu Y., Merrill T., Yildiz V., deSilva B., and Matrka L., “Risk Factors for Posttracheostomy Tracheal Stenosis,” Otolaryngology and Head and Neck Surgery 159 (2018): 698–704. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Fiacchini G., Trico D., Ribechini A., et al., “Evaluation of the Incidence and Potential Mechanisms of Tracheal Complications in Patients With COVID‐19,” JAMA Otolaryngology. Head & Neck Surgery 147 (2021): 70–76. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Fiacchini G., Proietti A., Poma A. M., et al., “Inflammatory Profiles of Tracheal Biopsies From SARS‐CoV‐2 Patients,” Frontiers in Microbiology 13 (2022): 851460. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Data S1. AAS-69-0-s001.docx (480.2KB, docx) Data Availability Statement The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.',97,'2025-10-15 11:04:30','2025-10-15 11:04:30');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5238,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3852440','Students benefit from developing their own emergency medicine OSCE stations: a comparative study using the matched-pair method.','Heinke W, Rotzoll D, Hempel G, Zupanic M, Stumpp P, Kaisers UX, Fischer MR.','2013','parallelized hinges mastered honing electrotherapy polytrauma polytrauma retrosternal polytrauma motionless motorcyclist visor colliding stifneck polytrauma morison koller epigastric trialled skillslab arrives jumping stifneck hypertonia parallelized familiarized furnished skillslab sounded manned pasw sigmastat coincidental defuses refuted outlay deters refresh monocentrically begging heinke heinke daisy rotzoll daisy rotzoll hempel hempel michaela zupanic michaela zupanic stumpp stumpp udo kaisers udo kaisers muenchen willingly iblher zupanic hartel parbel prußmann heinze schmucker anasth downie gleeson soboll holzer ausbild zma000703 moltner duelli resch ausbild doc44 timmermann angstwurm walcher 227x timmermann walcher angstwurm rettungsmed s10049 foos tkacz foos griggs erlbaum hamer luxton caspersen peerwise lernzielkatalog poliklinik intensivtherapie uniklinik fuer studenten lernzielkatalog selby nickendei ausbild bushnell cilliers schuwirth blume perleth ausbild ausbild buckingham srhe','Abstract Background Students can improve the learning process by developing their own multiple choice questions. If a similar effect occurred when creating OSCE (objective structured clinical examination) stations by themselves it could be beneficial to involve them in the development of OSCE stations. This study investigates the effect of students developing emergency medicine OSCE stations on their test performance. Method In the 2011/12 winter semester, an emergency medicine OSCE was held for the first time at the Faculty of Medicine at the University of Leipzig. When preparing for the OSCE, 13 students (the intervention group) developed and tested emergency medicine examination stations as a learning experience. Their subsequent OSCE performance was compared to that of 13 other students (the control group), who were parallelized in terms of age, gender, semester and level of previous knowledge using the matched-pair method. In addition, both groups were compared to 20 students who tested the OSCE prior to regular emergency medicine training (test OSCE group). Results There were no differences between the three groups regarding age (24.3 ± 2.6; 24.2 ± 3.4 and 24 ± 2.3 years) or previous knowledge (29.3 ± 3.4; 29.3 ± 3.2 and 28.9 ± 4.7 points in the multiple choice [MC] exam in emergency medicine). Merely the gender distribution differed (8 female and 5 male students in the intervention and control group vs. 3 males and 17 females in the test OSCE group). In the exam OSCE, participants in the intervention group scored 233.4 ± 6.3 points (mean ± SD) compared to 223.8 ± 9.2 points ( p < 0.01) in the control group. Cohen’s effect size was d = 1.24. The students of the test OSCE group scored 223.2 ± 13.4 points. Conclusions Students who actively develop OSCE stations when preparing for an emergency medicine OSCE achieve better exam results. Keywords: OSCE, Emergency medicine, Undergraduate education, Assessment of training Background Emergency medicine is an area where successful treatment hinges on clinical competence and practical skills. Since doctors are expected to perform efficiently in emergency medicine, practical essentials must be mastered by every doctor. One way of ensuring the necessary expertise is to test whether medical students meet the required standards of emergency medicine in practical exams like the objective structured clinical examination (OSCE) [ 1 ]. OSCEs are used to examine practical clinical skills, the application of procedural knowledge, and under certain circumstances the existence and honing of medical attitudes [ 2 - 4 ]. Moreover, OSCEs are a popular examination format among students [ 1 , 5 , 6 ]. Despite these advantages, OSCEs are not yet routinely used on medical students (including emergency medicine) in Germany [ 7 ]. Instead, students’ knowledge of emergency medicine continues to be solely assessed by means of multiple choice exams at 89% of German medical faculties [ 8 , 9 ]. OSCEs in emergency medicine are rarely used because they are labour-intensive and require large quantities of materials [ 8 ]. Numerous organizational and logistical problems need to be solved prior to an OSCE, such as devising a sufficient number of OSCE stations which reflect the syllabus and educational aims. Furthermore, the execution of an OSCE needs to be precisely planned: enough trained examiners must be on hand, the exam stations need to be prepared (e.g. provided with suitable simulation materials and consumables), and the results need to be evaluated in due time. The time and resources required for a medical school to prepare OSCEs could be reduced if students were involved in their planning. It has been reported that questions in MC exams which were developed by students improved their training and enabled them to achieve higher scores in both oral and written examinations [ 10 - 12 ]. This positive effect is mainly accounted for by students’ knowledge being activated by generating their own questions [ 10 ]. If this principle were applied to more complex types of examinations such as OSCEs, perhaps an even greater improvement in students’ learning and assessment performance could be achieved. Studies are needed to ascertain how students’ scores in a regular OSCE exam are affected if they voluntarily develop OSCE stations. If it turns out that developing OSCE stations results in better exam marks by activating their knowledge and skills, this would be a good argument for systematically involving students in the preparation of OSCEs. It would reduce faculties’ time and resources spent on preparing OSCEs while students could demonstrate improved practical skills in their exams. The aim of this study was to investigate whether the voluntary development of OSCE stations by students leads to better examination results. Given students’ improved MC exam results after producing their own MC questions, we expected to see a similarly positive impact on an OSCE after the voluntary development of OSCE stations. Methods Ethics committee, data protection Prior to the study, ethics approval was applied for from the Faculty of Medicine of the University of Leipzig’s ethics committee, who decided this study did not require special approval. Even so, for reasons of data protection, each participant gave written consent to their participation and to the anonymous evaluation and publication of the findings. Emergency medicine on the curriculum in Leipzig Emergency medicine is taught in Leipzig starting in the seventh semester. The syllabus is based on the learning objectives specified by the German Society of Anaesthesiology and Intensive Care [ 13 ]. Training comprises a series of 20 lectures on emergency medicine followed by a four-week PBL course (problem-based learning). This is designed to ensure that students first take theoretical instruction before undergoing extensive practical, clinically oriented training on the PBL course. During the Emergency Medicine PBL course, students work through eight paper and online cases in small groups facilitated by specially trained tutors. In addition, intensive training in emergency medical techniques takes place during the periods of practical training in Human Rescue, Emergency Room Management, Emergency Imaging and Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation under the guidance of university lecturers and emergency services staff. Parallel to this, during the four-week Emergency Medicine PBL course, students have the opportunity to consolidate their practical skills in additional voluntary training sessions run by faculty-trained student tutors. Until the 2010/11 winter semester, emergency medical training (the series of lectures and the PBL course) concluded with an MC test. In this particular semester, the MC exam was for the first time followed by an emergency medicine OSCE in order to test students’ practical skills. Development of an emergency medicine OSCE In the 2010/11 winter semester, an OSCE in emergency medicine was held for the first time at the University of Leipzig’s Faculty of Medicine. The examination topics were chosen and the OSCE stations devised by a team of anaesthesiologists, emergency physicians, cardiologists, radiologists, paediatricians and students based on both learning objectives in the syllabus [ 13 ] and a needs analysis of the targeted learners [ 14 ]. The content of the individual stations was worked out by the team’s medical members and verified by representatives of the disciplines involved. Scenarios and checklists for the individual stations were compiled jointly. All stations of the emergency medicine OSCE were tested by a group of 20 students before emergency medicine training started in order to check and, if necessary, modify the stations. The OSCE stations used in the emergency medicine exam are listed in Table 1 . Table 1. Brief description of the OSCE stations Station Setting, task Basic life support • An unconscious person (mannequin) has been discovered at a building site • Basic resuscitation Advanced life support • You are a member of a resuscitation team and are required to assist resuscitation (mannequin) • Pulse analysis, electrotherapy and drug treatment of cardiac arrest Insertion of a peripheral venous catheter in a polytrauma patient • Providing intravenous access in an unconscious polytrauma patient (mannequin arm) • Correct insertion using the right size peripheral venous catheter, selection of suitable infusion solutions Chest pain • 51-year-old patient with retrosternal pain at A&E • Establishing medical history, ECG evaluation, administration of drugs Bag valve mask ventilation • You are a member of a resuscitation team and responsible for securing the airway during resuscitation • Demonstrating the correct performance of bag valve mask ventilation (airway mannequin) Polytrauma: helmet removal and neck immobilization • Motionless motorcyclist (mannequin) with helmet still on and visor closed after colliding with a tree • Demonstration of the correct removal of the helmet and neck immobilization using Stifneck® Newborn resuscitation • Treatment of a baby born by emergency Caesarean (mannequin) in hospital • Evaluation and stimulation (if necessary resuscitation) of the baby Focused assessment with sonography for trauma • Polytrauma management in the casualty room (A&E, sonography training system) • Performance of emergency sonography showing Morison’s pouch, Koller’s pouch, epigastric region and heart, urinary bladder Preparing and carrying out blood transfusion • Postoperative tachycardia and anaemia in a coronary disease patient in the recovery room • Preparing and carrying out transfusion, demonstration of ABO compatibility test ECG • Patient chest pain on the left (mannequin) • Recording an ECG, arrangement of further diagnosis Open in a new tab The 10 stations described in the table were used in the summative OSCE. These stations were developed by the responsible teaching stuff of the faculty. However, some similarities exist compared to the stations developed by the students (see also Table 2 ). Study design Originally, two groups of students, an intervention group (n = 13) and a control group (n = 13), were formed in a matched-pair design to test the working hypothesis. Additionally, 20 students who had tested the emergency medicine OSCE prior to regular emergency training were included in the study and compared to the intervention group (test OSCE group, n = 20). Intervention group A total of 13 students (8 females, 5 males) were recruited for the intervention group who were in their seventh semester of medicine at the Faculty of Medicine at the University of Leipzig. These students voluntarily developed OSCE stations under the guidance of trained teaching personnel (first author, W.H.). At this time, none of the 13 students had any personal experience of either emergency medicine or the OSCE format. Two workshops were held to explain the structure of an OSCE, the examiner checklists and scoring. The students were asked to form groups in order to develop OSCE stations together. They set up eight OSCE stations to be trialled at Leipzig SkillsLab (see Table 2 ). The development process was supported by two authors (W.H., P.S.) of this study. Students received feedback on the stations they had developed: on medical aspects from the relevant teaching staff and on educational aspects from lecturers with a Master’s degree in Medical Education (W.H., P.S.). Table 2. Description of the OSCE stations developed by students Station Setting, exercise Airway/intubation • Discovery of a 50-year-old man showing no signs of life • Intubation to secure the airway Advanced life support • 60-year-old patient at the ward with cardiovascular arrest • Operation of a defibrillator Peripheral venous access • Insertion of a peripheral venous catheter (mannequin arm) in a 75-year-old female patient at an internal medicine ward • Assessment, preparation and insertion of intravenous access Angina • A 60-year-old man arrives at A&E by ambulance with severe chest pains • Focused case history, differential diagnosis of chest pain, clinical examination for chest pain Bag valve mask ventilation • Unconscious female on the floor • Demonstrating the correct performance of bag valve mask ventilation (airway mannequin) Neck immobilization • 26-year-old woman still conscious after jumping out of a window • Correct immobilization of the neck using Stifneck® Cardiopulmonary resuscitation of a baby • Anxious mother calls the doctor at the paediatric ward because her four-month-old child is not responding • Taking vitals of a baby (mannequin), resuscitation (one- and two-person) Central venous catheter • Patient with hypertonia and tachycardia suffering acute pancreatitis at A&E • Demonstration of insertion points of the central venous catheter, explanation of the procedure Open in a new tab Eight stations where the students tested each other were set up at Leipzig Training Clinic. The students also marked each other using the examiner checklists compiled before testing the stations that they had devised themselves in a simulated OSCE. Please note, none of these OSCE stations developed by students were used during the summative OSCE. Stations used in the summative OSCE were developed by the responsible teaching staff. All members of the intervention group used the eight OSCE stations they had developed to test each other’s knowledge of emergency medicine in a simulated OSCE. For this purpose, on the day of the simulated OSCE the students were given additional training in order to take on the role of the expert rater. This OSCE was held under the supervision of the responsible teaching staff (including some of the authors: W.H., G.H., D.R. P.S.) one week before the practical parts of students’ regular emergency training started. The participants of the intervention group had therefore not been trained in practical emergency medicine but had merely had the opportunity to attend emergency medicine lectures beforehand, where both their knowledge and the stations they had created were tested. Control group Thirteen students who took part in the first emergency medicine OSCE in the 2010/11 winter semester made up a control group in order to compare the exam results and test the working hypothesis. Instead of being chosen at random, the members of the control group were selected by using the matched-pair procedure in order to ensure their prior knowledge of general and emergency medicine were comparable to the intervention group. Students were therefore chosen from the same semester who had achieved the same score in the emergency medicine MC exam. Since there were several suitable control group candidates among the 321 OSCE participants, their gender and age were also parallelized (Table 3 ). This enabled each member of the intervention group to be matched to a student in the control group, producing a series of thirteen matched pairs. Table 3. Comparison of the scores of the intervention group and the control group Intervention group Control group Stud. Gender Age MC OSCE Gender Age MC OSCE 1 M 22 34 226.5 M 23 34 209.5 2 F 24 34 229.5 F 22 34 221.5 3 M 23 33 235.5 M 23 33 229.5 4 M 23 33 240.0 M 22 33 228.5 5 F 28 31 235.5 F 24 31 224.0 6 M 27 30 232.0 M 26 30 216.5 7 F 22 29 241.0 F 22 29 227.5 8 F 30 27 227.0 F 32 27 221.0 9 M 23 27 221.0 M 23 27 228.5 10 F 24 27 243.0 F 24 27 205.5 11 F 21 26 231.0 F 21 26 239.0 12 F 25 26 236.5 F 24 26 232.5 13 F 24 24 235.5 F 24 24 225.5 Mean 24 . 3 29 . 3 233 . 4 24 . 2 29 . 3 223 . 8 (SD) (2.6) (3.4) (6.3) (3.4) (3.2) (9.2) Mean comparison. t -test for paired samples p > 0.05 p > 0.05 p < 0.001 Effect size (Cohen’s d ) d = 1.24 Open in a new tab Demographic data and exam results of the student participants in the intervention group and the control group (Stud. = student number; SD = standard deviation, F = female, M = male). The maximum scores that could be reached were 250 points in the OSCE and 40 points in the MC test. Test OSCE group This group served as control to exclude the advantage of the intervention group, who became familiarized with the OSCE format by trying out their stations in a simulated OSCE. Therefore, we compared the intervention group to a group of students who completed the emergency OSCE before regular emergency training started (see above). This group was formed for two reasons. Firstly, we wanted to test the suitability of our OSCE. Secondly, we wanted to demonstrate the efficacy of our four weeks’ practical training in emergency medicine. Therefore, 20 voluntary students of the 7th semester took the emergency OSCE one week before regular emergency training started (i.e. at the same time as the student-authored OSCE stations were tested by the intervention group in their simulated OSCE). In order to avoid a familiarity bias with the OSCE format, we compared the OSCE scores of the intervention group to the scores of these 20 students (= test OSCE group) obtained during the regular exam OSCE. OSCE The emergency medicine OSCE consisted of ten stations (Table 1 ). Eight stations were provided with simulation models (insertion arm for peripheral venous access, a mannequin for basic and advanced life support, a sonography torso, a mannequin of a newborn, an ECG training mannequin, and a mannequin for neck immobilization) while two stations were furnished with examination and diagnostic materials (ECG evaluation, blood transfusion). The OSCE circuit was set up twice for the exam, enabling 20 students to be examined at once. The study participants were randomly assigned to one of the OSCE circuits by the staff of the registrar’s office, who were not involved in the study (i.e. the circuits were randomly filled with participants of the three study groups; participants completed either circuit 1 or 2). The exam was held at Leipzig SkillsLab since it contains a series of differently equipped rooms (outpatient room, sonography room, etc.), allowing clinical events to be realistically simulated. None of the stations developed by the intervention group were used for this summative OSCE. At each station, the students had five minutes in which to perform the tasks set. One minute was allowed to move from one station to the next. Students were also given a minute before the first station to familiarize themselves with the tasks. The total duration of the exam was hence 60 minutes. A starting signal and a time’s up signal were sounded at each station. Each day, 120 students were examined by junior doctors, specialists and consultants from various departments of the Faculty of Medicine. Examiners at the individual stations were chosen based on their specializations. Radiologists for instance were in charge of the emergency sonography station (FAST), trauma surgeons manned the neck immobilization station, and paediatricians handled the newborn resuscitation station. They were given thorough training in their examining duties beforehand. This began with a 45-minute theoretical training session on OSCE for all expert raters one week before the OSCE. On the day before the OSCE, once the OSCE course had been completely set up, the raters were trained at their stations. A responsible lecturer demonstrated the performance expected of examinees at each station. The raters were also shown how to use the checklists or to make a global rating. On stations with global rating, the raters were given written guidelines on how to award points in order to minimize interrater differences. The raters were given a final briefing 30 minutes before the start of the OSCE. This training was intended to minimize raters’ impact on the test results. In addition, after each round of tests, the points awarded at each station were reviewed by the first author of this study (W.H.). If any anomalies were found (e.g. above-average or exceptionally low scoring), the raters were given additional training. Students’ performances were assessed by the examiners with checklists [ 15 , 16 ]. Usually five tasks had to be performed at each station. As the maximum score at each station was 25, the maximum possible total score of the OSCE was 250. Depending on the station and the exercise, scoring was done by using either a global rating or a checklist rating [ 16 ]. The OSCE was carried out as a compensatory examination (i.e. examinees did not have to pass every station – instead, their exam grade depended on their total score) and the pass rate was set at 60% [ 16 ]. The complete OSCE was tested before regular emergency training was started by 20 students (test OSCE group, see above) and faculty staff. Statistics The data were logged and processed in Microsoft Excel. Afterwards, group comparisons were carried out. In order to ascertain the effect of students actively developing OSCE stations, the total scores in the OSCEs of the three groups were compared by means of an analysis of variance followed by Bonferroni correction for multiple comparisons. Moreover, a direct comparison between the intervention and control groups was carried out as originally planned with respect to matched pairs design, i.e. the OSCE scores of both groups as well as the results at the individual stations were compared by means of Student’s t -test if the data were normally distributed and the Mann–Whitney U test for non-normally distributed data. Results below a significance level of p < 0.05 were rated as significant. Finally, Cohen’s d as effect size was calculated in order to compare the scores achieved in the OSCE. The data were evaluated using the software suites PASW Statistics 18.0 and Sigmastat (Version 2.0). Results Demographic data and previous knowledge The average age of the students in the intervention group was 24.3 ± 2.6 (mean ± standard deviation) years. As a result of the matching procedure, the mean age of the control group (24.2 ± 3.4 years) and its gender distribution were no different from the intervention group ( t -test for paired samples, p > 0.05; Table 3 ). The students of the test OSCE group had the same mean age (24 ± 2.3 years) but significantly different gender distribution (3 male, 17 female vs. 5 male, 8 female). Comparing their MC results by means of a one-way ANOVA revealed no differences between the three groups ( p > 0.05). Students in the intervention group scored 29.3 ± 3.4 points, those in the control group achieved 29.3 ± 3.2 points, while the test OSCE group achieved 28.9 ± 4.7 (see Table 4 ). In addition, a t -test for paired samples comparing the intervention group and the control group with respect to the matched pairs design revealed no differences between the two groups ( p > 0.05; Table 3 ). Table 4. MC and OSCE scores of the investigated groups Intervention group (n = 13) Control group (n = 13) Test OSCE group (n = 20) Mean ± SD Max Min Mean ± SD Max Min Mean ± SD Max Min OSCE 233.4 ± 6.3 243 221 223.8 ± 9.2 239 205.5 223.2 ± 13.4 239.5 179.5 MC 29.3 ± 3.4 34 24 29.3 ± 3.2 34 24 28.9 ± 4.7 36 20 Open in a new tab MC and OSCE scores of the Interventions group, Control group and test OSCE group (SD = standard deviation, Min = minimum score, Max = maximum score). The differences between the intervention group and control group as well as between the test OSCE group are significant (p < 0.05). Comparison of overall results During the OSCE, the students in the intervention group scored 233.4 ± 6.3 points, on average 9.6 higher than their counterparts in the control group (223.8 ± 9.2). The students in the test OSCE group scored 223.2 ± 13.4 points in the regular exam OSCE. A one-way ANOVA with factor group revealed significant differences between the three groups ( p = 0.024). The pairwise multiple comparison procedure (Bonferroni t -test) showed significant differences between the intervention group and the test OSCE group ( p = 0.031) and nearly significant differences between the intervention group and control group ( p = 0.082). Again, we compared the scores of the intervention group and the control group by means of the t -test for paired samples because these two groups were matched regarding the number of participants, age and gender. Table 3 displays these results. The mean difference of 9.6 points in the OSCE is significant ( t -test for paired samples, p < 0.01) with a large effect size of Cohen’s d = 1.24 (d ≥ 0.8 = large effect). The other participants of the emergency medicine OSCE of the 7th semester (n = 275) who were not in any of the three groups scored on average 216.6 ± 16.5 points. Since only some of the student-authored stations were similar to stations that were used in the OSCE, the individual stations were compared for the intervention group and the control group. The aim was to determine whether the learning improvements stemming from station development were only related to the specific stations used in the OSCE or whether devising OSCE stations as a whole improves students’ mastery of practical emergency medicine. Note that because the students and teaching staff developed OSCE stations independently, the similarities between the simulated OSCE and the exam OSCE stations were coincidental. Scores at the individual OSCE stations Means (± SD) of the scores achieved at the 10 stations of the OSCE compared between the intervention group (blue) and the control group (orange). Statistical differences are marked by an asterisk. They can be substantiated for the venous access and basic life support stations ( p < 0.05). The maximum possible score at each station was 25 points. Figure 1 shows a clear trend with the students in the intervention group scoring better than the control group at all stations. Although this effect can be statistically demonstrated for the basic life support ( p = 0.043) and venous access ( p = 0.037) stations, the differences at the other individual stations were not significant ( t -test for paired samples at normal distribution, Mann–Whitney U test with non-normal distribution, p > 0.05). Figure 1. Open in a new tab Scores at the individual OSCE stations. Significant differences are marked by an asterisk. Discussion This paper explores the hypothesis that students perform better in OSCEs if they develop OSCE stations. This hypothesis was confirmed since the students in the intervention group achieved significantly higher exam marks than those in both comparison groups (the control group and the test OSCE group). As the participants in all groups had scored the same results in the previous MC exam in emergency medicine, this improved performance cannot be attributed to a higher level of previous knowledge in the intervention group. This defuses the argument that those developing OSCE stations in this study were higher performing, more enthusiastic students. Moreover, the argument that familiarity with the OSCE format or practice at a number of stations may have resulted in a generalizable improvement is refuted by the better results of the intervention group than the test OSCE group. Instead, the intervention group’s better exam results can most likely be explained by the greater amount of time they spent addressing practical content of emergency medicine. This positive learning effect has already been described for students generating MC questions and is therefore referred to in this context as a ''generation effect’ [ 10 , 11 ]. The hypothesis that the intervention group students did better in the exam because they had dealt with practical aspects of emergency medicine more intensively is backed up by the fact that they tended to score higher at all OSCE stations (Figure 1 ). In fact the members of the intervention group had begun working on practical elements of emergency medicine two months before the start of emergency medicine training according to the syllabus. In our view, the main effect of the teaching intervention is therefore accounted for by the intervention group’s active, more thorough study of practical emergency medicine overall and is not related to a contamination bias due to previously known OSCE stations. Future studies could quantify the learning effort by using learning diaries to better understand the process induced by the intervention [ 17 ]. The key finding of our study is its potential impact on training. Students who are interested in assessment-related content (such as emergency medicine in our study) could systematically be involved in the development of OSCE stations early on, as they would benefit both during their initial medical degrees and in their subsequent specialist training. A recent quantitative study proposed a framework for the factors impacting on students’ pre-assessment learning [ 18 ]. However, this model does not take the preparation of assessment materials itself into account and it might be supplemented by this task type in ''the creation of assessment items’ in the future. Another advantage of encouraging students to develop OSCE stations is the potential to cut the faculty’s workload in preparing OSCEs. After all, one reason why OSCEs are currently rarely used in Germany is probably because they tie up considerable time and resources [ 7 ]. The logistical and financial outlay deters many faculties from holding OSCEs, especially since the resources required for oral and MC exams are comparatively low [ 19 , 20 ]. But the preparatory work for an OSCE could be minimized if students were involved in its planning, especially the development of scenarios and stations. OSCE stations could be developed not just by students preparing for the regular emergency medicine exam but also by more senior students (e.g. during their internships) in order to refresh their skills and knowledge in emergency medicine. Although not stated as a research question, another interesting finding of our study is the similarity of the faculty-developed OSCE and student-developed OSCE stations. Six stations were nearly identical (advanced life support, peripheral venous access, bag valve mask ventilation, neck immobilization, chest pain and newborn reanimation). Merely two stations designed by the students were not regarded as essential by the faculty staff (insertion of a central venous line and endotracheal tube insertion). The similarity between OSCE stations reflects the ''constructive alignment’ of the emergency medicine curriculum. Learners construct their own learning through relevant learning activities. The teacher’s job is to create a learning environment that supports the learning activities appropriate to achieving the desired learning outcomes. The key is that all components in the teaching system – the curriculum and its intended outcomes, the teaching methods used and the assessment tasks – are geared to each other [ 21 ]. Moreover, in our opinion the quality of the student-developed OSCE stations did not differ substantially from the faculty-developed ones. This statement might, however, be a little speculative since there was no deliberate, standardized analysis of quality or similarity. This point should be addressed by future studies. Nevertheless, these findings further underline the potential of involving interested students in the development of their assessment. Limitations of the study In the final analysis, it remains difficult to distinguish whether generalizable improvement results from more practice on a number of stations or spending more time on practical aspects of emergency medicine by students creating OSCE stations. This is especially true for the comparison between the intervention and control group. However, the fact that the students of the intervention group scored better than the test OSCE group clearly supports the argument that a positive learning effect arose from the voluntary creation of OSCE stations. Alternatively, the study could be repeated with experienced test-takers rather than OSCE novices, because the practice effect on OSCE novices is probably bigger. Moreover, the study was conducted monocentrically and only for an OSCE in emergency medicine, begging the question of its validity for other medical content domains. However, since the ''generation effect’ has been proven for MC questions [ 10 , 11 ], the generation of OSCE stations can be assumed to bring about a similar effect. Nevertheless, for these reasons – and also because the number of participants in the intervention group was relatively low – follow-up studies need to be carried out to confirm the findings, preferably in other areas of medicine and with more experienced test-takers than in our study, because the practice effect on OSCE novices is probably greater. Conclusions Students who themselves develop OSCE stations in emergency medicine perform better in the subsequent clinical summative OSCE. This finding is a good argument for involving interested students in the development of their assessment. Competing interests The authors declare that they have no competing interests. Authors’ contributions WH participated in the conception and design of the study, carried out the data acquisition. In addition he participated in the analysis and interpretation of data and he drafted the manuscript. DR and GH participated in the data acquisition. MZ participated in the conception and design of the study as well as in the analysis and interpretation of data. PS and UK critically reviewed the manuscript. MF participated in the conception and design of the study as well as in the analysis and interpretation of data. He critically reviewed the manuscript. All authors read and approved the final manuscript. Pre-publication history The pre-publication history for this paper can be accessed here: http://www.biomedcentral.com/1472-6920/13/138/prepub Contributor Information Wolfgang Heinke, Email: wolfgang.heinke@medizin.uni-leipzig.de. Daisy Rotzoll, Email: daisy.rotzoll@medizin.uni-leipzig.de. Gunther Hempel, Email: gunther.hempel@medizin.uni-leipzig.de. Michaela Zupanic, Email: Michaela.Zupanic@uni-wh.de. Patrick Stumpp, Email: patrick.stumpp@medizin.uni-leipzig.de. Udo X Kaisers, Email: udo.kaisers@medizin.uni-leipzig.de. Martin R Fischer, Email: Martin.Fischer@med.uni-muenchen.de. Acknowledgements We wish to thank the students who participated in the study, and gave their time and intellectual input willingly and gratefully. References Iblher P, Zupanic M, Härtel C, Parbel A, Prüßmann M, Heinze H, Fischer M, Schmucker P. Prepared for an emergency? An OSCE in medical student training in emergency medical care. Anästh Intensivmed. 2011;52:812–822. [ Google Scholar ] Harden RM, Stevenson M, Downie WW, Wilson GM. Assessment of clinical competence using objective structured e