ge.2017.03.024. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 53. Ribaldi F, Altomare D, Jovicich J, et al. Accuracy and reproducibility of automated white matter hyperintensities segmentation with lesion segmentation tool: a European multi-site 3T study. Magn Reson Imaging. 2021;76:108–15. 10.1016/j.mri.2020.11.008. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 54. Shirzadi Z, Schultz SA, Yau WYW, et al. Etiology of White Matter hyperintensities in autosomal Dominant and sporadic Alzheimer Disease. JAMA Neurol. 2023;80(12):1353–63. 10.1001/jamaneurol.2023.3618. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 55. Huang D, Guo Y, Guan X, et al. Recent advances in arterial spin labeling perfusion MRI in patients with vascular cognitive impairment. J Cereb Blood Flow Metab. 2023;43(2):173–84. 10.1177/0271678X221135353. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 56. Raghavan S, Przybelski SA, Reid RI, et al. White matter damage due to vascular, tau, and TDP-43 pathologies and its relevance to cognition. Acta Neuropathol Commun. 2022;10(1):1–14. 10.1186/s40478-022-01319-6. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 57. Dewenter A, Jacob MA, Cai M, et al. Disentangling the effects of Alzheimer’s and small vessel disease on white matter fibre tracts. Brain. 2023;146(2):678–89. 10.1093/brain/awac265. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary Material 1 (3.9MB, docx) Data Availability Statement The data analyzed for the current study are available from the CIEN Foundation but restrictions apply to their availability due to personal data protections reasons. Anonymized data are however available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.',412,'2025-10-15 11:04:54','2025-10-15 11:04:54');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5246,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6179650','No longer waiting for an accident to happen: Simulation in emergency medicine.','Ellison S, Sullivan C, McCullough R.','2013','truman umkc truman umkc truman umkc youngblood ymsl wappingers metiman meti sarasota simman wappingers simman metiman realizes thoracostomy thoractomy peridcaridocentesis intraosseous intraosseous germane youngblood metiman youngblood simman wappingers classmates umkc kagaku kagaku umkc simwars simwars umkc simman simman kagaku umkc plains simman cites promotions stefanie msma msma truman truman ellisonst umkc lammers chakravarthy haar binstadt nadel takayesu pozner acgmeweb tabid programandinstitutionalguidelines basedaccreditation emergencymedicine emergencymedicineguidelines satish streuferts healhtcare qhc hobgood promes moriarity quaintance e228 okuda bonfante e19 e40 nasca philibert nejmsr1200117 tintinalli shofer biese phipps rabinovich s71 s78 practic bandali','Abstract The practice of emergency medicine (EM) requires proficient and expert skills in multiple high risk procedures. The emergency physician in-training needs a safe and realistic environment in which to practice and perfect the skills necessary to care for patients ranging from the critically ill to the patient with difficult intravenous access. Undergraduate medical education overall has a need for training that enables students to develop the knowledge, skills and attitudes to practice in a variety of specialties. This article provides an overview of simulation in a three-year emergency medicine residency at Truman Medical Center, in a required final year clerkship for all medical students at the University of Missouri-Kansas City, and discusses national trends for the use of simulation in emergency medicine. Introduction Simulation is the artificial structuring of a situation, environment, or experience for the purpose of learning, evaluation, or skills training. 1 This can include high-fidelity mannequins, task trainers, standardized patients or professionals, or a combination of these tools to meet the teaching and assessment needs of the experience. The practice of emergency medicine is ideally suited to this form of training to teach and assess the merging of decision-making and procedural skills because the specialty frequently requires recognition and action of life-saving skills such as, an open thoracotomy or the intubation of a patient in respiratory distress. Simulation education is well established in EM residency training and is expanding in scope and practice in undergraduate medical education, especially in EM student clerkships. 2 In the past, residents in emergency medicine were trained in an apprenticeship model where they learned about procedures through didactic lectures and reading and then practiced their skills by treating patients under supervision and performing procedures when necessary but often in high risk environments. 1 , 3 Skills training and judgment occurred on real patients and in a high stress environment. For more than a decade, residency programs and training facilities have focused more on efficiently training residents and the need to integrate an ever growing body of knowledge to practice in the field. Simulation also suits the growing need to provide more active learning for knowledge acquisition, medical decision-making and skills development. Additionally, the Council of Residency Directors have recognized the need for alternative and clinically oriented teaching tools for assessment in the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) competencies. 4 Residents and medical students report increased confidence and satisfaction with the use of simulation in their training. 5 , 6 For residents, realistic patient experiences can provide relevance and context to learning, including improving retention of the material. 7 An undergraduate medical education program is ideally suited for simulation to function as a teaching tool for evaluating and training students, especially as medical education is evolving to more clinically oriented case-based scenarios and small-group didactic sessions. Studies have demonstrated the effectiveness of simulation in teaching basic science, clinical knowledge, communication skills, procedural skills, and team-based care. 2 Simulation activity for training of medical students and residents in emergency medicine is growing and is essential at the University of Missouri-Kansas City (UMKC) School of Medicine and at the Truman Medical Center emergency medicine residency training program. UMKC-Truman Medical Center Emergency Residency Program The emergency medicine residency training program utilizes simulation to teach and assess residents in acquiring the knowledge, medical decision-making skills, and to practice skills to prepare them in the care of patients. The residency curriculum is broken down into the following categories: Simulation Experiences Advanced Cardiac Life Support (ACLS) training utilizes computerized simulation and task trainers to provide training and certification to all learners. Each year, post graduate year one (PGY-1) emergency medicine residents obtain ACLS provider and instructor certification during their residency orientation month. The UMKC Youngblood Medical Skills Laboratory (YMSL) is the site for ACLS certification for senior medical students and all specialty residents. Emergency Medicine residents serve as ACLS instructors for these courses throughout their three-year training program. This model promotes residents as teachers which also facilitates the maintenance of these skills by our residents. As instructors, residents use basic airway Laerdal® Airway Management Trainer (Laerdal Wappingers Falls, NY) and CPR models along with high-fidelity computer-integrated mannequins (METIman® mannequin simulator, Medical Education Technologies, Inc, METI, Sarasota, FL and Laerdal SimMan®, Laerdal, Wappingers Falls, NY) to teach and assess basic and advanced resuscitative techniques. This expertise is carried further as EM residents are looked to for guidance not only in the emergency department, but when they respond to medical codes throughout the hospital. Advanced Trauma Life Support, Pediatric Life Support, and Basic Life Support certification also utilize simulation and are required certifications for all EM residents. An Emergency Medical Services curriculum has also been adapted and implemented for the 2012–2013 academic year as a three-year longitudinal experience for residents. Throughout training, EM residents will teach and learn alongside Emergency Medical Technician (EMT) and Paramedic students in their training program at the School of Medicine. This is an inter-professional component of the residency curriculum that allows these learners to develop skills in simulated patients utilizing the programmed Laerdal SimMan® and METIman® cases that further reinforces their roles and responsibilities and the teamwork of their future practice. As residents progress in their skill level, they will have an increasing role in teaching the EMT and paramedic students. This curriculum includes objectives based on post-graduate level and assessment in-line with the ACGME competencies. There are many additional simulation experiences in the course of the academic year. Some examples include advanced airway skills and adjuncts, procedural sedation, central line placement, and lumbar puncture. Quarterly, residents work with pediatric emergency medicine faculty using high-fidelity mannequins to manage critical pediatric cases, maternal and neonatal resuscitation, as well as essential procedures on size appropriate models. Ultrasound education focuses on interpreting images and enhancing techniques, and allows for practice both on procedural models as well as resident peers. Future simulation experiences include the evaluation of residents’ skills in hand-over communication (transition in patient care) using standardized health care team members. Cadaver Laboratory Despite advances in simulation technology, the residency program leadership realizes that cadavers provide a more authentic, anatomically detailed simulation experience for certain procedural skills. A three-week annual cadaver lab for high acuity, low frequency procedures is provided to all residents. The curriculum endeavors to assess procedural competency, namely knowledge, skills and attitudes of learners. Procedures practiced in the curriculum include: retrograde wire intubation, open and percutaneous technique for cricothyrotomy, chest tube thoracostomy, thoractomy, peridcaridocentesis, diagnostic peritoneal lavage, and intraosseous access. An introductory session prior to the laboratory emphasizes each procedure’s indication, contraindications, key anatomy, equipment, and methods to confirm success. Residents are encouraged to do further reading on each procedure and must successfully pass a knowledge test to participate in the lab. Residents receive one-on-one faculty observation during all procedures with immediate verbal as well as written checklist feedback. Residents can practice each skill multiple times and incorporate faculty feedback as they repeat the skills. The cadaver lab supports the patient care, medical knowledge, and practice-based learning and improvement competencies. Emergency Medicine Required Clerkship All medical students at the University of Missouri-Kansas City School of Medicine are required to complete a one-month emergency medicine clerkship in the last eighteen months of medical school, and have done so for greater than 30 years. As a final year clerkship, it is essential to enable the student to evaluate the undifferentiated patient, learn critical patient management principles, and learn the skills necessary to perform procedures such as endotracheal intubation, lumbar puncture, central line placement, and intraosseous line placement, all germane to the practice of emergency medicine. Experiential Learning During orientation, students are introduced to a systems-based approach to the undifferentiated and sometimes critical patient. Students are provided an approach to identify problems with “airway, breathing, and circulation” and an interactive discussion of management principles occurs. The objective of this session is to teach students skills on how to recognize and manage the technical aspects of critical illness. This activity also stresses techniques on how to communicate with the critically ill patient, the patient’s family, consultants, and the health care team -- skills they will need as resident physicians, regardless of specialty. Students are then asked to manage eight “paper” cases as a team where they are provided the chief complaint and vital signs and then discuss the differential diagnosis, physical examination, diagnostic tests and procedures for each patient. Students are also instructed on critical communication skills to ensure that they safely and efficiently care for these patients. This approach has been detailed in a previous manuscript. 8 Following this activity, students are required to observe a critical patient encounter during the month and document on a worksheet the airway, breathing and circulatory status of the patient, interventions of the healthcare team, and list the tests and procedures that lead to all decision-making until that patient is stable or admitted. This exercise guides the experiential learning of the student and reinforces the management principles taught throughout the month. Simulation Experiences In the first week of the clerkship, students attend an airway skills training session in the Youngblood Medical Skills Laboratory on basic and advanced airway maneuvers. This lab includes a didactic discussion of indications, contraindications, and complications of placement of various oxygen delivery devices, oral airways, nasal airways, bag-valve-mask techniques, endotracheal intubation, and laryngeal mask airway placement (See Figure 1 ). Students practice each of these skills and complete this lab early in the clerkship so that they learn and practice essential skills prior to observing them during critical encounters in the ED. The Laerdal® Airway Management Trainer and the METIman® mannequin simulators are used during this simulation. Figure 1. Open in a new tab In the first week of the clerkship, students attend an airway skills training session in the Youngblood Medical Skills Laboratory on basic and advanced airway maneuvers. This lab includes a didactic discussion of indications, contraindications, and complications of placement of various oxygen delivery devices, oral airways, nasal airways, bag-valve-mask techniques, endotracheal intubation, and laryngeal mask airway placement. Students also attend a 90-minute simulation skills session with faculty where students manage two critical cases that are programmed into the Laerdal SimMan® (Laerdal, Wappingers Falls, NY) to reinforce concepts learned in the classroom and during patient care. Students individually are asked to manage the patient scenario using the systematic approach learned in the classroom. Students make clinical decisions about airway, breathing and circulation management, establish intravenous access, and practice ACLS treatment protocols while their classmates observe. They can ask faculty and their peers for assistance. The simulation is designed to provide experiential learning in order to reinforce the medical decision-making, deliberate practice, and management principles from the didactic and interactive lectures for the month. Procedural Curriculum In 2008–2011, two faculty developed and implemented a multi-session, interactive procedural curriculum to teach two procedures, internal jugular central line and lumbar puncture, during the emergency medicine clerkship. This curriculum was funded internally by the UMKC School of Medicine dean’s fund. Our goal was to teach task training along with decision-making so that students have deliberate practice to achieve competence in the performance of high-risk procedures. The first session consisted of a pre-test, a one-hour interactive PowerPoint presentation about each procedure, and introduction of a checklist for each procedure. The session ended with a step-by-step demonstration by the faculty using the Blue Phantom Internal Jugular Central Line® (Blue Phantom, Redmond, WA) and the Kyoto Kagaku lumbar puncture simulator II® (Kyoto Kagaku Co., Ltd Head Office, Japan 612-8388) low-fidelity simulators. Each student then practiced and performed the procedure and observed other students while faculty provided instruction and feedback. In the second week, students were given a case-based, online tutorial outlining procedural complications, video instruction of the procedure, and a review of the checklist. In the third week, students worked through two interactive computerized cases requiring management decisions and recognition of indications for each procedure. As a capstone experience, in the final week students managed one of two critical patients programmed into a high-fidelity simulator. In the course of management, the student would decide that one of the procedures was clinically indicated, perform that procedure and be evaluated by faculty based on the skills checklist. Students then took a post-test and evaluated the curriculum. Ninety-two students completed all elements of the curriculum and the results showed that students demonstrated improvements in both cognitive and mechanical aspects of these procedures. Student evaluations showed a high level of satisfaction with the content and skills they learned in participating in the curriculum. This curriculum is ideally suited for the final year medical student and can be adapted to a residency program, but it takes faculty time for adequate teaching and assessment during a one-month clerkship. Students with an interest in emergency medicine at the UMKC School of Medicine have assembled a student SIMWARS team for the current academic year. Simulation teams have convened on the regional and national level in conferences of The Society for Academic Emergency Medicine and this year at the national meeting marks the 8 th annual SIMWARS competition between residents. Residents and faculty in emergency medicine have designed a simulation training track at UMKC and are continuing to train the student team. This training has utilized cases programmed in Laerdal SimMan® as the simulated patient and incorporates the use of standardized professionals, as well as the use of task trainers to practice and demonstrate procedural airway skills (Laerdal® Airway Management Trainer and SimMan®), central line placement (Blue Phantom Internal Jugular Central Line®), and lumbar puncture (Kyoto Kagaku lumbar puncture simulator II®). Training has also included EMS students to highlight a collaborative team approach to patient care. The four-student team represented the UMKC SOM and lost in the final round to the champions in the Great Plains regional SAEM conference in September 2012. Residents and students are planning the upcoming training schedule and plan to compete again this year. This is a great opportunity for students and residents to learn and practice the team-based approach to care. National Simulation Applications In 2008, greater than 90% of emergency medicine programs in the United States reported the use of simulation to train their residents while 85% reported specifically using mannequin-simulators. 9 (See Figure 2 .) Most residency programs have added simulation to a well-designed curriculum with a focus on medical decision-making and procedural skills training. A comprehensive medical simulation education curriculum for emergency medicine residents was introduced by Brigham and Women’s Hospital in 2005 and reported it in 2007. 2 Their model involved a complete curriculum redesign to create a fully integrated medical simulation model that included adult learning principles, medical simulation learning theory, and standardized curriculum requirements to create a set of simulation-based modules. They utilize computer simulation, an advanced skills laboratory, human patient simulation, and classroom techniques to integrate simulation across the continuum of residency education. Figure 2. Open in a new tab The Laerdal ® SimMan ™ patient simulator is used for clinical skills teaching including airway, breathing, cardiac and circulation management, and wound assessment / care. It features heart, bowel, respiratory and vocal sounds, and trauma modules can also be used with this simulator. The 2011 Model of the Clinical Practice of Emergency medicine was released in July of this year and represents the fifth review of the EM Model. 10 This model cites a listing of medical knowledge, patient care and procedural skills essential to the fundamental treatment for patients that present to emergency departments and is based on data collected by the National Center for Health Statistics at the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. It is representative of the core knowledge and skills that are required of the emergency medicine physician to provide safe and effective patient care. The section on procedural skills is comprehensive, containing 67 procedures in all and including airway techniques, resuscitation skills, anesthesia and pain management, diagnostic and therapeutic procedures by system, and other procedures including ultrasound. As many of the model’s listed procedures are infrequently performed, the need to maintain procedural competency through simulation is of increasing importance. The ACGME will implement The Next Accreditation System (NAS) in July 2013. A key component will be the reporting of individual resident educational milestones. 11 Previous work has demonstrated that there is considerable variation between residents’ exposure to clinical patient care encounters relevant to the practice of emergency medicine. 12 Simulation may have an increasing role to ensure that resident learners experience all types of patient care scenarios in appropriate frequency as well as to document competency of educational milestones. For undergraduate medical education, graduate medical education and across professional schools, simulation provides an ideal interprofessional education (IPE) opportunity. The World Health Organization recently described IPE and collaborative practice to mean: “Interprofessional education occurs when students from two or more professions learn about, from, and with each other to enable effective collaboration and improve health outcomes. IPE is a necessary step in preparing a ‘collaborative practice-ready’ health workforce that is better prepared to respond to local health needs.” 13 Simulation also supports enhanced teamwork and encourages patient safety between professionals through deliberate practice of communication skills, including the opportunity to learn and practice the roles and responsibilities inherent to professionals as they contribute to the team-based care of a patient. As the focus has shifted to patient-centered care and team-based care, health professionals will need to learn with, from and about one another in real-life settings in order to facilitate teamwork and collaboration. 14 Providing this IPE is an essential next step for all professional schools and simulation of patient care scenarios that combine these learners in realistic scenarios will reinforce experiential learning in the same context of how the learners will practice. We predict that simulation will be an important and innovative tool to provide IPE in the areas of patient safety, communication, and improved health care delivery. Summary Simulation-based education is fundamental to an emergency medicine residency program and the emergency medicine clerkship. It fosters a safe environment to develop medical decision-making skills, diagnosis and management skills, and essential procedural skills in graduate and undergraduate medical education. Future directions will be to develop simulations as educational milestones measures in residency training and possibly for medical student promotions in a medical education program. Ideally simulations will be added as experiential learning activities in courses and clerkships across the medical education program since there is a growing emphasis on active learning for the adult learner in residency training and in medical school curricula. Simulation is an ideal activity to assist learners with integration of basic science knowledge and clinical medicine, with motor skill development of clinical skills and procedures, and it can also provide the learner with the context to better understand systems-based physiology and pathophysiology in developing a diagnostic and treatment plan for patient care. Simulation is also an opportunity to provide interprofessional education and training in the areas of patient safety, communication, and team-based care. Biography Stefanie Ellison, MD, MSMA member since 2012, (top) is an Associate Professor and Emergency Medicine Clerkship Director, and Christine Sullivan, MD, MSMA member since 1983, (left) is an Associate Professor and Emergency Medicine Residency Program Director. Both are in Department of Emergency Medicine at Truman Medical Center. Robert McCullough, MD, (right) is a PGY-2 Emergency Medicine Resident at Truman Medical Center. Contact: ellisonst@umkc.edu Open in a new tab Open in a new tab Open in a new tab Footnotes Disclosure None reported. References 1. Lammers RL. Simulation: The New Teaching Tool. Annals of Emergency Medicine. 2007;49(4):505–507. doi: 10.1016/j.annemergmed.2006.11.001. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Chakravarthy B, Haar E, et al. Simulation in medical school education: review for emergency medicine. West J Emerg Med. 2011 Nov;12(4):461–466. doi: 10.5811/westjem.2010.10.1909. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Binstadt ES, Walls RM, White BA, Nadel ES, Takayesu JK, Barker TD, Nelson SJ, Pozner CN. A Comprehensive Medical Simulation Education Curriculum for Emergency Medicine Residents. Annals of Emergency. 2007 Apr;49(4):495–504. doi: 10.1016/j.annemergmed.2006.08.023. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Accreditation Council of Graduate Medical Education Outcome Project Web site. General competencies. [Accessed September 10, 2012]. Available at http://www.acgme.org/acgmeweb/tabid/292/ProgramandInstitutionalGuidelines/Hospital-BasedAccreditation/EmergencyMedicine/EmergencyMedicineGuidelines.aspx . 5. Weller JM. Simulation in undergraduate medical education: bridging the gap between theory and practice. Med Educ. 2004;38:32–38. doi: 10.1111/j.1365-2923.2004.01739.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Satish U, Streuferts S. Value of a cognitive simulation in medicine: towards optimizing decision making performance of healhtcare personnel. Qual Saf Health Care. 2002;11:163–167. doi: 10.1136/qhc.11.2.163. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Hobgood C, Promes S, Wang E, Moriarity R, Goyal DG. Outcome assessment in emergency medicine—a beginning: results of the Council of Emergency Medicine Residency Directors (CORD) emergency medicine consensus workgroup on outcome assessment. Acad Emerg Med. 2008;15(3):267–277. doi: 10.1111/j.1553-2712.2008.00046.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Ellison SR, Sullivan C, Quaintance J, Arnold L. Critical Care Recognition, Management and Communication Skills during an Emergency Medicine Clerkship. Med Teach. 2008;30(9–10):e228–38. doi: 10.1080/01421590802334259. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Okuda Y, Bond W, Bonfante G, et al. National Growth in Simulation Training Within Emergency Medicine Residency Programs 2003–2008. Acad Emerg Med. 2007;49:505–507. doi: 10.1111/j.1553-2712.2008.00195.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. 2011 Model EM. [Accessed Sept 2012];Academic Emergency Medicine. 2012 Jul;19(7):e19–e40. doi: 10.1111/j.1553-2712.2012.01385.x. http://www.abem.org/PUBLIC/portal/ [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Nasca TJ, Philibert I, Brigham T, Flynn TC. The next GME accreditation system—rationale and benefits. NEJM. 2012;366:1051–1056. doi: 10.1056/NEJMsr1200117. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Tintinalli JE, Shofer F, Biese K, Phipps J, Rabinovich S. Toward a new paradigm: goal-based residency training. Acad Emerg Med. 2011;18:S71–S78. doi: 10.1111/j.1553-2712.2011.01174.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. World Health Organization. Framework for Action on Interprofessional Education & Collaborative Practic. 2010. [accessed August 2012]. http://www.who.int/hrh/professionals/activities/en/index.html . 14. Robertson J, Bandali K. Bridging the gap: Enhancing interprofessional education using simulation. Journal of Interprofessional Care. 2008;22(5):499–508. doi: 10.1080/13561820802303656. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',7,'2025-10-15 11:04:57','2025-10-15 11:04:57');
