aths involving very high-range alcohol concentrations. Addiction. 2013;108:1411–1417. doi: 10.1111/add.12191. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Drummer OH. The Forensic Pharmacology of Drugs of Abuse. London, UK: Arnold; 2001. [ Google Scholar ] 23. Janssen F, Kunst AE. ICD coding changes and discontinuities in trends in cause-specific mortality in six European countries, 1950–99. Bull World Health Org. 2004;82:904–913. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Lu T-H, Lunetta P, Walker S. Quality of cause-of-death reporting using ICD-10 drowning codes: a descriptive study of 69 countries. BMC Med Res Methodol. 2010;10:30. doi: 10.1186/1471-2288-10-30. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Rainio J, Kultti J, Kangastupa P, Tuomi H, Ahola S, Karhunen PJ, et al. Immunoassay for ethyl glucuronide in vitreous humor: a new tool for postmortem diagnostics of alcohol use. Forensic Sci Int. 2013;226:261–265. doi: 10.1016/j.forsciint.2013.01.040. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. McDonald H, Borinskya S, Kiryanov N, Gil A, Helander A, Leon DA. Comparative performance of biomarkers of alcohol consumption in a population sample of working-aged men in Russia: the Izhevsk Family Study. Addiction. 2013;108:1579–1589. doi: 10.1111/add.12251. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Rainio J, Ahola S, Kangastupa P, Kultti J, Tuomi H, Karhunen PJ, et al. Comparison of ethyl glucuronide and carbohydrate-deficient transferrin in different body fluids for post-mortem identification of alcohol use. Alcohol Alcohol. 2014;49:55–59. doi: 10.1093/alcalc/agt159. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, National Center for Health Statistics. Instructions for classifying the underlying cause of death. 2013. Available at: http://www.cdc.gov/nchs/nvss/instruction_manuals.htm (accessed 12 November 2013) (Archived at http://www.webcitation.org/6NnYthpMQ on 3 March 2014)',22,'202