INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5254,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10293024','Barriers and Best Practices for the Use of Patient-Reported Outcome Measures in Emergency Medicine.','Lin MP, Kligler SK, Friedman BW, Kim H, Rising K, Samuels-Kalow M, Eucker SA.','2023','thrombolytic proliferated multistep unaligned lacerations chores momentary dvprs bpi unidimensional unidimensional unidimensional unidimensional pyelonephritis fractured variously photophobia phonophobia bothersome literate reframing dialects dialectical miscommunications transcribing subscribers technologically literate oswestry unidimensional boateng rmdq concisely afeqt sustainably rmdq timescales belly dialect dialects dialects hl143042 agewall borggrefe commonwealthfund mercieca bebber stockler talkingquality mmshub masterson creber spadaccio dimagli fremes brinker kyte wees hove tolan grogan jayakumar laverty s38 bouazza chiairi kharbouchi bausewein benalia cry qualityforum reported_outcomes_environmental_scan oppen alshibani vaillancourt dainty golozar cowley ader lanoue gentsch spritzer schalet norquist girman fehnel demuro mercon santanello milosevic phobias clio dvprs dvcipm dvprs poquet bpi kaushik sauro measuringu espinola kosowsky menditto maraldo barbadoro sajobi lix munjal bothersome glazier moineddin schull burstin krieg hudon chouinard demo sehsd wp2021 ibraheim rajakulendran culpan guirguis raney e397 fayers aaronson schechter mulvey esses bijur jbw acquadro bayles juniper pneumol dialects aschmann ameng wustl cult liebert ameringer conor bojarski meirte hellemans anthonissen perioper e15588 pewresearch projectredcap lizzio gulledge franovic makhni arthrosc e535 reinke mather nla lever marcellus muschong e061283 lanoue gerolamo doty kalow henien vaillancourt dainty nct04349020 boateng neilands frongillo melgar quinonez banzett parsall gracely schwartzstein lansing zazzali lavallee chenok graupner kimman mul blumenstein halpenny','Abstract Patient-reported outcome measures (PROMs) are commonly used in clinical trials and have been incorporated into routine clinical care in select specialties but have not been widely implemented in emergency medicine research and clinical care. We describe measurement-related barriers to PROM use in the ED; administrative and practical considerations; implications of developing novel EM-specific PROMs; and key considerations for the use of PROMs in emergency medicine research, clinical care, quality measurement, and outcome improvement. Despite the unique barriers of the ED environment, potential solutions include the use of ED-validated PROMs when available; adapting existing short-form, multi-dimensional PROMs previously validated in diverse populations, ideally using computer-adapted testing; and collecting responses during anticipated wait times. With this work, we aim to inform barriers and best practices to use of PROMs in emergency medicine research and clinical care to support future more widespread implementation of PROMs within emergency care. The successful adoption of PROMs for diverse ED patient populations within the unique constraints of the acute care environment may help researchers, clinicians, and policymakers improve the quality and patient-centeredness of acute care. BACKGROUND: Patient-reported outcomes (PROs) are increasingly recognized as a critical component of evaluating quality and effectiveness of patient care. 1 – 3 PROs are broadly defined as a “report of the status of a patient’s health condition or health behavior that comes directly from the patient, without interpretation of the patient’s response by a clinician or anyone else”. 4 Patient-reported outcome measures (PROMs) are instruments that have been developed to ensure valid and reliable measurement of these PROs. PROMs often measure constructs that are not captured in existing data sources, such as symptoms (pain, dyspnea), functional status, and quality of life. Health care quality measures have traditionally focused on structures, processes, and outcomes of care defined by providers and policymakers – for example: time to thrombolytic administration for patients with acute ischemic stroke (process) or readmissions within 30 days after inpatient discharge (outcome). 5 In contrast, PROMs are driven by outcomes that patients identify as their highest priorities; offer the ability to quantify subjective patient data; and allow for the evaluation of outcomes directly related to patients’ reasons for seeking care. PROMs have been identified by policymakers and regulatory bodies including the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services as a key priority in quality measurement; however, little is known about the use of PROMs in emergency department (ED) settings. 6 Currently, PROMs are widely used in research and clinical care across other specialties including orthopedic surgery, cardiology, physical rehabilitation, oncology, and palliative care, among others. 7 – 13 However, the use of PROMs in EDs remains limited by numerous barriers including the lack of valid and reliable PROMs for emergency care and guidance on how best to adapt PROMs to the ED setting. 14 Existing PROMs that are applicable to the ED focus primarily on measuring patient experience and patient satisfaction after ED encounters. While such measures of satisfaction and experience have proliferated, they primarily reflect processes of care rather than results of care and may not be the outcomes of greatest importance to ED clinicians or patients. 15 OBJECTIVE: A key barrier to expansion of PROMs in the ED setting is limited understanding of how best to operationalize use of PROMs in the ED for research and clinical care. This concept paper aims to address this critical gap by describing barriers and best practices for the implementation and use of PROMs in emergency medicine research and clinical care. We begin with (1) a discussion of measurement barriers, including challenges to the optimal identification of PROMs for emergency care, followed by (2) practical and administrative barriers to the collection of PROMs in the ED setting; (3) barriers to the development of novel EM-specific PROMs; and (4) current recommendations and future considerations for the adoption of PROMs in clinical care and quality measurement in emergency medicine. SECTION 1: MEASUREMENT BARRIERS 1a. Barriers to the Identification of ED PROMs Incorporating PROMs into emergency medicine research and clinical practice can have multiple purposes: to assess patient engagement, identify treatment goals, guide clinical decision-making centered on patients’ values and preferences, measure outcomes of importance to patients, and improve quality. The selection of instruments varies depending on the intended purpose. Identifying outcomes of importance to ED patients is the first step towards measuring PROMs in ED settings, but the optimal approach is unclear. 16 Outcomes prioritized by patients may include but are not limited to existing PROMs – for example, mortality is not a patient-reported outcome, but is an outcome highly prioritized by most patients. Historically, outcomes of importance are identified through a multistep process of (1) eliciting key concepts through qualitative interviews, (2) organization of elicited concepts into domains corresponding to measurable constructs or outcomes, and (3) prioritization of concepts and outcomes. 17 Given the time and resource-intensive nature of qualitative methods, group concept mapping and group brainstorming have been recently demonstrated as more efficient approaches for capturing patient priorities. 18 This work should be extended to include diverse stakeholders, for example those with limited health literacy and caregivers of ED patients with cognitive impairment. 1b. Timeframe of Existing Scales Unaligned with Acute, Time-Sensitive ED Care One major issue with using existing PROMs in the ED is that most have not been developed to be administered during timeframes relevant to an ED visit. In particular, patients may present to the ED for evaluation within minutes to hours after onset of symptoms (e.g., injuries immediately after a motor vehicle crash, or acute onset chest pain). However, most existing PROMs have been developed for assessing progression and treatment of chronic conditions during longitudinal care in outpatient clinic settings. 19 The majority of PROMs evaluate average severity of the outcome of interest over the past 7–14 days, making them difficult to use for symptoms of shorter duration and insensitive to change over the minutes-to-hours time scale of ED encounters. 20 In addition, while these types of PROMs may be important to patients with subacute or chronic conditions, they may be less relevant for more self-limited problems, such as simple lacerations or acute viral syndromes. Furthermore, some measures such as quality of life or functional measures of daily activity (e.g., performance of household chores) are difficult to assess or detect changes in during the timeframe of the ED visit, as often the patient has not yet attempted these functional domains. The 0–10 pain numeric rating scale for assessing current pain score and the Subjective Units of Distress Scale (SUDS) for measuring current anxiety level are among the few measures designed for assessing rapid changes; however, momentary assessments are less stable measures of symptom severity. 21 Other more comprehensive acute measures designed for 24 hour time-scales include the Defense and Veterans Pain Rating Scale (DVPRS) and Brief Pain Inventory (BPI) adapted for 24 hours, which are both measures of pain and its interference with mood, function and daily activities. 22 , 23 However, little is known regarding the optimal timing or mode of collection during the ED encounter to evaluate the impact of ED care on patients’ symptoms and function. 1c. Many Existing PROMs are Unidimensional Most existing instruments are unidimensional, meaning they measure only one outcome or construct. For example, many scales in the NIH-funded Patient-Reported Outcome Measure Information Systems (PROMIS) repository are focused on a single symptom (e.g., dyspnea) or outcome (e.g., self-efficacy for managing medications), and most short-from PROMs include at least 4 questions for the reliable measurement of each outcome. 24 However, patients often prioritize multiple outcomes during an ED visit; for example, patients with abdominal pain may prioritize pain relief, reduction in nausea severity, and improved diagnostic certainty. In these situations, multiple instruments measuring different outcomes are needed, increasing the response burden and complexity of measurements. Further psychometric analysis may reduce the minimum number of questions necessary to evaluate each outcome, but this can vary based on outcome complexity. For example, pain severity can be assessed with a single question while quality of life requires multiple questions. 25 Unidimensional PROMs may be appropriate in cases where there is only one outcome of interest; in other cases, collecting multiple short-form unidimensional PROMs or using multidimensional PROMs may be necessary. 1d. Challenges Identifying PROMs for Acute, Undifferentiated Illness and Injury As most PROMs are diagnosis-specific, another major challenge to their use is the undifferentiated nature of acute conditions evaluated in the ED. Often, patients do not receive a diagnosis until the very end of their ED visit, and one third of discharged ED patients leave without a clear pathologic diagnosis. 26 For example, patients who present to the ED with back pain may have a kidney stone, pyelonephritis, fractured vertebrae, or musculoskeletal back pain. Therefore, PROMs focused on musculoskeletal back pain may not address all the needs of many ED patients with back pain. One approach may be to identify symptom-based conditions commonly evaluated in ED settings, such as chest pain, abdominal pain, injury, shortness of breath, and then develop and validate multidimensional PROMs for these conditions. For example, a multidimensional chest pain PROM may encompass improvement of the primary and associated symptoms, uncertainty and fear regarding diagnosis, and self-efficacy regarding follow-up plan. Another approach is to use existing instruments developed for specific symptoms. 1e. Patient Priorities Differ, Even for the Same Condition For a given chief complaint, even when a diagnosis has been established, different patients may have different priorities when seeking emergency care that necessitate different PROMs. 27 One paradigm is to use a combination of PROMs assessing both general outcomes (e.g. overall quality of life) for comparisons across disease states, and disease-specific outcomes that may be more sensitive to disease severity and impact. Furthermore, including a relative importance scale would allow patients to identify the outcome(s) they are prioritizing at the time of PROM collection. 28 For example, patients with migraine may variously identify photophobia, nausea, or phonophobia as their Most Bothersome Symptom (MBS), with differences recently noted by demographic group. 29 While relative importance scales may add to response burden, incorporating a relative importance scale into a computer-adaptive testing system may actually help streamline collection of PROMs in the order prioritized by patients as a potential future solution. 1f. Existing PROMs Not Validated in ED Populations One key barrier to using existing PROMs in emergency care is that few have been developed or validated in ED patient populations. Many PROMs were developed in populations that routinely attend outpatient clinic appointments for chronic medical conditions and preventative care. These populations may be more educated, affluent, or health literate than patients seeking care in EDs. 19 , 30 – 33 As a result, the wording of questions and answer choices may not be consistently understood by people of different cultural, socioeconomic or educational backgrounds. 34 , 35 However, re-wording or reframing questions may impact the measurement characteristics of the original PROM, necessitating additional validation. Validation within a given population can be time and labor intensive as well as costly, require large sample sizes – up to 200–300 people per demographic characteristic (e.g., language, educational status), 36 and involve time-intensive cognitive interviews. Thus, utility of existing PROMs in EDs is often extrapolated from similar or representative non-ED populations, typically without formal validation. 37 , 38 A pragmatic solution to collect PROMs in the ED may be to use measures validated in diverse populations that are as similar to the target ED population as possible. In addition, most PROMs are English-only, with few originating from or translated into other languages. Translating PROMs is a similar intensive, multi-step validation process to ensure consistency of content and measurement properties across languages. 4 , 39 Even among English speakers, interpretation may differ between countries and American dialects. 40 Linguistic concordance is rarely addressed in discussions of language validation, but dialectical differences between patient and clinician may contribute to miscommunications in both face-to-face and electronic interactions. 41 While language and linguistic barriers are not unique to the ED, the prevalence of uninsured ED patients and the association of uninsured status with limited English proficiency make this issue particularly relevant in emergency care. 42 , 43 SECTION 2: ADMINISTRATIVE BARRIERS TO THE COLLECTION AND ADOPTION OF PROMS 2a. Managing Response Burden Many PROMs are lengthy and consist of several questions to evaluate a single construct to ensure reliable psychometric properties. Given the time-sensitive nature of emergency care, the potential for high acuity illness, and the pressure to maintain patient throughput, brief PROMs are generally preferred in the ED setting. An example is the Roland Morris Disability Questionnaire comprised of 24 questions assessing symptoms and activity interference related to musculoskeletal back pain validated and commonly used in clinic settings, which has been adapted to a concise 5-question version validated for ED use. 37 However, concise scales are not always available or validated, may not fully capture outcomes prioritized by individual patients, may be less sensitive to detect low magnitude differences, and may be less generalizable across diverse patient groups. Furthermore, multiple short scales are needed to capture multiple dimensions, such as the PROMIS-29 concise measure of seven health domains. Perceived response burden can be decreased by delivering PROMs during natural wait times in the ED; for instance, while patients are in the waiting room or waiting for test results, discharge paperwork, or transport to an inpatient floor. In our experience, the collection of short PROMs during expected waiting times of the ED encounter can maximize response rates and minimize response burden. 2b. Post-ED PROM Collection: Balancing Timing with Response Rate Given the time limitations associated with collecting PROMs in the ED, one solution for using existing PROMs to assess acute episodes of care is to measure outcomes after patients leave the ED. However, post-encounter PROM collection is resource-intensive and associated with rapidly diminishing response rates over time, which can introduce selection and non-response bias. 44 Addressing these issues by collecting PROMs during subsequent ambulatory care visits is not always feasible among ED patients who have greater barriers to outpatient follow up. Additionally, the impact of post-ED care (e.g., primary care follow-up) can make PROM responses over time less specific to the ED care delivered. Conversely, because EDs serve as an integral hub for patients entering the healthcare system and accessing care, using PROMs to assess combined ED management with subsequent care pathways may be at least as important as assessing ED management alone. The optimal approach depends on the desired objective: PROM collection prior to ED discharge may be optimal to maximize response rate, while post-ED collection may be employed if longer time periods are needed to assess the outcome of interest and resources are available to collect PROMs after the ED encounter. 2c. Mode of PROM Collection The various modes of PROM delivery, which include in-person vs remote, and verbally vs paper vs electronic systems vs electronic health record (EHR)-based, all have trade-offs. While these concerns are not unique to emergency care, the need to limit response burden in the fast-paced ED environment must be balanced with the inclusion of populations with lower health and technology literacy who may be more likely to seek care in the ED. 45 Electronic collection of PROMs can improve the time-burden and accuracy of data collection compared to verbal collection, as well as decrease the time required for data entry compared to paper collection and transcribing into an electronic database. 46 Remote electronic systems have the potential to broaden delivery beyond the healthcare encounter and are frequently more time and cost-effective, but require technological access and literacy that may present barriers for some individuals. Recent advances to reduce these barriers include broadening accessibility to smartphones and other mobile devices that are much more ubiquitous than computers alone. 47 Large secure databases such as REDCap make it easier for researchers to utilize common PROMs with a minimum of effort and resources. For example, PROMIS-29 is a commonly used concise multi-dimensional PROM that has been added to the shared REDCap library and is available to all institutional subscribers. 48 However, the use of PROMs from shared libraries relies on the integrity of coding in the public version and may be limited to select languages, limiting the ability of users to adapt to the needs of local populations. 49 PROMs that are integrated into the EHR and sent automatically to patients may improve the scalability of measuring these outcomes in a large number of patients; however, the trade-off may be lower response rates and bias towards a population which is more technologically literate, has more interest in or time available for answering questionnaires, and/or has a clinician advocate, among other characteristics. 50 Conversely, in-person or telephone administration of PROMs can address many of the technological barriers, have much higher completion rates, and are able to reach more socioeconomically disadvantaged individuals, but are also more time- and resource-intensive, which may not be practical for many studies. 51 Thus, we recommend collecting PROMs electronically while providing an option for in-person or telephone administration when resources are available. 2d. Computer-Adaptive Testing The use of computer-adaptive testing (CAT) can further streamline PROM collection and reduce respondent burden, as these methods can adjust the questions delivered to patients based on their responses to earlier questions, eliminating some questions and presenting others to both reduce the total number of questions and improve the accuracy of the measure through targeted questioning. They can also be delivered electronically, in-person or by telephone. In an ongoing randomized controlled trial of ED patients with low back pain, 125 respondents required a median of 0.78 minutes to complete the PROMIS-Pain Interference CAT (median of four items generated), compared to a median of 2.90 minutes to complete the 10-item Oswestry Disability Index. 52 SECTION 3. BARRIERS TO DEVELOPING NOVEL EMERGENCY CARE PROMS Given the challenges to using previously developed PROMs in ED populations, one potential solution is to develop novel PROMs specifically designed for use in the ED. However, PROM development is time-consuming and resource-intensive: it entails a multi-step process of eliciting outcomes of importance and refining questions through qualitative work with patients, then quantitative internal and external validation incorporating psychometric analysis and requiring large sample sizes. 17 Several efforts have been recently completed or are currently underway to develop PROMs for ED patients, although none have been widely utilized thus far. Three examples include: (1) a novel unidimensional scale that was developed to measure patient uncertainty related to symptoms, which has previously been shown to be a driver of ED utilization, 53 (2) substantial work performed to elicit outcomes and begin development of a multi-dimensional scale among adult and pediatric asthma patients, and (3) the multi-dimensional PROM-ED which captures several constructs prioritized by ED patients, including symptom relief, understanding of their health concern, reassurance, and having a plan. 54 – 56 While the PROM-ED holds promise as a measure designed specifically for ED patients experiencing acute, undifferentiated conditions, it has only been validated in a relatively small, homogeneous patient population. Novel PROMs developed and intended for use among ED patients may be more valid and reliable for the measurement of acute care outcomes, but may not link as well to longitudinal care outcomes. In particular, it is unlikely that new multidimensional ED PROMs would replace disease- and symptom-specific PROMs that have already been adopted – and in some cases widely used for performance and reporting – in many outpatient settings. As Boateng et al note: “Thousands of measures/scales have been developed to measure a range of psychological, social, physical and health behaviors, experiences, and outcomes.” 57 Given the resource-intensive nature of PROM development and the fact that so many measures already exist, developing new measures may be less important and practical than adapting and/or validating existing measures to the ED environment. SECTION 4. RECOMMENDATIONS AND CONSIDERATIONS FOR THE ADOPTION OF PROMS IN EM RESEARCH AND QUALITY IMPROVEMENT Despite these challenges, PROMs are important to include in ED research. When possible, PROMs should be previously validated in ED populations, or populations as close to the target population as possible. When the diagnosis is apparent, disease-specific PROMs may be collected. For undifferentiated illness, symptom-based PROMs, such as the Dyspnea-12 and multidimensional dyspnea profile may be appropriate. 58 Care should be taken prior to PROM selection to ensure outcomes are prioritized by patients. Lessons learned from the collection and reporting of PROMs in emergency medicine research can and ideally should be translated to clinical care, quality improvement, and community health surveillance. 59 Once a PROM has been validated, outcome measurements should be provided to providers and patients to drive the improvement of care. Incorporating PROMs into care delivery to evaluate symptoms, inform treatment goals, and measure outcomes has been shown to improve outcomes including improved symptom control, health-related quality of life, patient activation and engagement. 60 , 61 Patients who take the time to complete PROMs want to know their providers are receiving their responses in a timely and actionable manner. With respect to mode of reporting PROMs, ongoing work in oncology demonstrates displaying PROMs in electronic health record dashboards is feasible and acceptable to clinicians; however, there is no literature to date on whether and how PROM dashboards can be adopted in the setting of acute episodic care. 62 In parallel, ongoing work has demonstrated the feasibility and acceptability of displaying PROM responses to patients. One approach for incorporating clinically meaningful information from PROs into quality improvement is to collect PROMs routinely used in other ambulatory care settings for acute exacerbations of chronic illness (ex. PHQ-9, RMDQ). While these instruments were not designed with acute care in mind, responses collected in the acute care setting may be compared to prior responses in the clinic setting and be reported to patients’ longitudinal providers to inform chronic disease management and prevent future ED visits and hospitalizations. Similarly, PROM responses at post-ED follow-up visits may be compared to the responses provided during the acute care encounter and serve as feedback to ED providers. Future work could engage ED patients experiencing conditions of interest, ED clinicians, primary care providers, and disease-specific specialists in the prioritization and adaptation of commonly used ambulatory care PROMs for acute ED use. CONCLUSION The use of PROMs in ED research and clinical care is challenging and requires careful consideration of barriers inherent to the unique ED environment, measurement properties of PROMs, and needs of diverse patients. Despite these challenges, PROMs previously used for the clinical condition of interest and validated in as similar a population as available, with attention to a range of outcomes covered as concisely as possible, are important to include in ED research. The successful adoption of PROMs in emergency care research can inform future efforts to incorporate PROMs into ED clinical care and quality improvement. The recommendations in this manuscript may be incorporated into future efforts by researchers, clinicians, and leaders to advance the patient-centeredness and quality of emergency care. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Examples of Existing PROMs Adapted for Common ED Conditions. * This figure illustrates how some commonly used PROMs might be adapted to ED care; however, many were not developed or validated for ED care. Given the paucity of PROMs designed for post-ED outcomes, consider selecting PROM with a time scale 