INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5257,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9612796','Cancer diagnosis is associated with a lower burden of dementia and less Alzheimer''s-type neuropathology.','Karanth SD, Katsumata Y, Nelson PT, Fardo DW, McDowell JK, Schmitt FA, Kryscio RJ, Browning SR, Braithwaite D, Arnold SM, Abner EL.','2022','karanth kcr kcr freestanding kcr kcr kcr hippocampi siptw siptw conditionally siptw siptw siptw kcr kcr unstageable siptw nominally ording ording igap complementarily deregulated amyloidogenic taxanes kcr kcr awac035_supplementary_data kcr siptw shama karanth gainesville gainesville yuriko fardo jaclyn markey dejana gainesville gainesville gainesville susanne markey ag028383 ag072946 eldik ag038651 r56 ag057191 fardo weuve scherr danaei hoorn folia beiser e1442 musicco adorni benito bermejo pareja nedices frain neurocogn mezencev chernoff sundquist sundquist ording veres puho seddighi pharoah pdp glymour e2025515 catala pinilla pinilla abdiwahab e196160 ackley ording puho veres tirumalasetti birkett yarchoan biogerontology boullosa tabares seisdedos baudot e1004173 caulfield rouanet memento mandelblatt luta yencilek yildirim slattery murtaugh wekstein kcr uky kcr uky karanth karanth replicable immunocytochemistry helzer mercaldo neuropsychologic winblad kivipelto pulliam neuropsychiatr 119b rejective statist iptw sampathu ubiquitinated haneuse schildcrout breitner pin1 nls cwc pin1 ap4 pin1 pastorino prolyl isomerase pin1 jazvinscak jembrek slade simic nudelman risacher saykin ostendorf bilanovic adaku epothilone ahles awac035_supplementary_data','Abstract Cancer and Alzheimer’s disease are common diseases in ageing populations. Previous research has reported a lower incidence of Alzheimer’s disease-type (amnestic) dementia among individuals with a diagnosis of cancer. Both cancer and amnestic dementia are prevalent and potentially lethal clinical syndromes. The current study was conducted to investigate the association of cancer diagnosis with neuropathological and cognitive features of dementia. Data were analysed from longitudinally evaluated participants in a community-based cohort study of brain ageing who came to autopsy at the University of Kentucky Alzheimer’s Disease Research Center. These data were linked to the Kentucky Cancer Registry, a population-based state cancer surveillance system, to obtain cancer-related data. We examined the relationship between cancer diagnosis, clinical dementia diagnosis, Mini-Mental State Examination scores and neuropathological features using inverse probability weighting to address bias due to confounding and missing data. To address bias due to inclusion of participants with dementia at cohort baseline, we repeated all analyses restricted to the participants who were cognitively normal at baseline. Included participants ( n = 785) had a mean ± standard deviation age of death of 83.8 ± 8.6 years; 60.1% were female. Cancer diagnosis was determined in 190 (24.2%) participants, and a diagnosis of mild cognitive impairment or dementia was determined in 539 (68.7%). APOE ɛ4 allele dosage was lower among participants with cancer diagnosis compared to cancer-free participants overall ( P = 0.0072); however, this association was not observed among those who were cognitively normal at baseline. Participants with cancer diagnosis had lower odds of mild cognitive impairment or dementia, and higher cognitive test scores (e.g. Mini-Mental State Examination scores evaluated 6 and ≤2 years ante-mortem, P < 0.001 for both comparisons). Cancer diagnosis also associated with lower odds of higher Braak neurofibrillary tangle stages (III/IV) or (V/VI), moderate/frequent neuritic plaques, moderate/frequent diffuse plaques and moderate/severe cerebral amyloid angiopathy (all P < 0.05). By contrast, TDP-43, α-synuclein and cerebrovascular pathologies were not associated with cancer diagnosis. Cancer diagnosis was associated with a lower burden of Alzheimer’s disease pathology and less cognitive impairment. These findings from a community-based cohort with neuropathological confirmation of substrates support the hypothesis that there is an inverse relationship between cancer and Alzheimer’s disease. Keywords: Cancer, Alzheimer’s disease, neuropathology, amyloid-β, neurofibrillary tangles In a post-mortem study of longitudinally evaluated individuals, Karanth et al. show that cancer diagnosis is associated with a lower burden of Alzheimer’s disease pathology and less cognitive impairment. The study thus provides neuropathological confirmation of an inverse relationship between cancer and Alzheimer’s disease. Introduction Cancer and Alzheimer’s disease are common diseases in ageing populations. Both cancer and Alzheimer’s disease have high morbidity and mortality, and are leading causes of death among older adults. 1 In the USA, over 5 million people currently have dementia, and its prevalence is expected to grow to 13.8 million by 2050. 2 Cancer and Alzheimer’s disease share many reported or hypothesized risk factors including higher age, lower education, sedentary behaviour, smoking and high-fat diet. 3 , 4 Yet, several studies, 5–15 including a Mendelian randomization study 16 and meta-analyses, 17–19 have reported lower incidence of Alzheimer’s disease and other neurodegenerative disorders among populations with a history of cancer compared with cancer-free controls. One study reported that patients with cancer had 21% lower risk of dementia compared to cancer-free controls; the risk of dementia was also lower in the cancer group before the diagnosis of cancer. 14 Another study reported that older individuals who developed cancer had better memory performance and slower memory decline than did similarly aged individuals who remained cancer free. 20 While such observed associations may be due at least in part to populations with cancer being less likely to survive to ages where dementia is most common, a simulation study showed that the competing risk of death before dementia and selective survival after cancer could not fully explain the inverse cancer-dementia association. 21 Evidence against the inverse association comes from a large Danish study of non-melanoma skin cancer and dementia risk among adults age 18 and older, where the inverse association was small and diminished over time. 22 While brain autopsy findings have not been reported extensively in the context of cancer phenotypes, the inverse association of cancer and Alzheimer’s disease was first suggested by a cross-sectional autopsy study. 23 More recently, an autopsy-based study showed that individuals with a history of cancer had reduced odds of developing clinical Alzheimer’s disease and a lower burden of neurofibrillary tangles compared to individuals with no history of cancer, but similar levels of amyloid-β. 24 No other neurodegenerative pathologies have been assessed in relation to cancer in previous literature. Previous research suggests that a matrix of shared genetic factors may confer risks of cancer and neurodegenerative disease in opposing directions. 25 , 26 For example, the apolipoprotein E ( APOE ) gene is the most impactful known genetic risk-contributing factor for Alzheimer’s disease, with ɛ4 alleles that are common—conferring strongly increased risk. 27 , 28 Yet, few studies examining the association between cancer and Alzheimer’s disease have accounted for APOE ɛ4 carrier status. 6 , 24 , 29 , 30 Moreover, the APOE ɛ4 allele has been suggested to have a protective role in some cancers. 31 , 32 Given the recently increased appreciation of the high prevalence of Alzheimer’s disease-mimicking conditions (pathologies associated with amnestic dementia, other than Alzheimer’s per se ), it is timely to reassess the specificity of the relationship between cancer and brain pathology. A key question is whether the inverse association of cancer history and clinical diagnosis of dementia, as shown by previous studies, is supported by neuropathological evidence. The primary objectives of the current study are to evaluate the relationships between cancer diagnosis, various neuropathological changes and final syndromic cognitive diagnosis. Materials and methods Study participants Data were drawn from the community-based cohort study of ageing and dementia at the University of Kentucky Alzheimer’s Disease Research Center (UK-ADRC). 33 , 34 All included participants enrolled from 1984 to 2017 and were ≥60 years at death. The University of Kentucky Institutional Review Board approved all UK-ADRC study procedures, and all participants provided written informed consent. Cancer ascertainment The Kentucky Cancer Registry (KCR) is a population-based central cancer registry for the Commonwealth of Kentucky. 35 KCR is part of the comprehensive National Cancer Institute’s Surveillance, Epidemiology and End Results (SEER) program. Kentucky law requires all health care facilities that either diagnose or treat cancer patients, as well as freestanding treatment centres, non-hospital (private) pathology laboratories and physician offices to report every case of cancer to KCR. 36 KCR started collecting uniform, high-quality data in 1995, so UK-ADRC participants ( n = 38) who died before 1995 were excluded. 35 The UK-ADRC autopsy data were linked to KCR data 36 to identify cancer diagnoses occurring in Kentucky from 1995 to 2017 and to acquire cancer subtype-specific data on diagnosis, stage, treatment and year at diagnosis. For the current study, only the first primary diagnosis identified by International Classification of Disease codes for Oncology 3rd Edition (ICD-O-3) 36 was considered a cancer case. Thus, each participant can count as only one cancer case but may have had multiple cancer diagnoses. The participants with an ICD-O-3 code for cancer were coded as 1 = cancer diagnosis and 0 = no cancer diagnosis. Cancer stage at diagnosis was categorized by the Summary Stage 2000 as in situ (non-invasive malignant tumour), localized (tumour is confined to the organ of origin), regional (tumour has spread by direct extension to immediately adjacent tissues, organs or lymph nodes) and distant (a tumour that has spread beyond the immediately adjacent tissues and has developed secondary or metastatic tumours). 37 Treatment was categorized into three levels as ‘no treatment’, ‘surgery or chemotherapy (and with or without radiotherapy)’ and ‘surgery with radiotherapy (and with or without other therapy)’. Additionally, to assess the timing of the cancer diagnosis relative to UK-ADRC participation, cancer cases were categorized as ‘diagnosis before ADRC enrolment’, ‘during the ADRC follow-up period’ or ‘diagnosed after the last recorded ADRC visit’. Because tobacco use is a leading risk factor for certain types of cancer, 38 we further classified cancer cases into smoking-related (oropharynx, oesophageal, liver, stomach, pancreas, lung, colorectal, kidney and urinary bladder) 38 and non-smoking-related cancers. Neuropathological assessment Details of neuropathological assessment at UK-ADRC have been described previously. 33 , 34 , 39–41 Neuropathological assessments were performed blind to clinical information. For the current study, Braak neurofibrillary tangle (NFT) stages were operationalized in grades of severity as Braak NFT stages 0/I/II, III/IV and V/VI. 42 Consortium to Establish a Registry in Alzheimer’s Disease (CERAD) diffuse and neuritic plaque ratings were categorized into dichotomous variables as moderate/frequent versus none/sparse. 43 Transactive response DNA-binding protein 43 kDa (TDP-43) proteinopathy was considered present if TDP-43 inclusion bodies were detected in the hippocampus, whereas Lewy bodies (α-synuclein) were considered present if detected in the brain stem, medial temporal lobe or the neocortex. In addition to neurodegenerative pathologies, subtypes of cerebrovascular pathology included measures of cerebral amyloid angiopathy, categorized as moderate/severe versus none/mild, atherosclerosis severity at the circle of Willis (all vessels ≥50% versus <50% occluded), any infarcts/lacunes (yes versus no) and brain arteriolosclerosis (moderate/severe versus none/mild). Cerebral atrophy was classified as moderate/severe versus none/mild. Additionally, both right and left hippocampi were evaluated for hippocampal sclerosis; presence on either side was considered as the presence of hippocampal sclerosis. Neuropsychological testing and cognitive status Cognitive function was evaluated on an approximately annual basis as described previously 44 , 45 with neuropsychological tests including Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE). 46 Participants were classified as having normal cognition, mild cognitive impairment (MCI), impaired cognition (but not MCI) or dementia at each study visit based on cognitive test scores, co-participant reports, neurological examination, medical history and physical examination. 47 Impaired cognition was defined as per the Uniform Data Set (UDS) standard protocol 47 and includes mild impairment not suspected to be due to neurodegenerative or cerebrovascular disease. Normal cognition indicates intact functional ability 47 and performance within expected ranges for age and education on neurocognitive tests. 48 MCI indicates the presence of objective impairment [scores >1.5 standard deviations (SD) below the expected mean] in one or more cognitive domains, or cognitive complaint, intact global cognition, no or minimal functional impairment and no evidence of dementia. 49 The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, 4th Edition (DSM-IV) criteria were used to determine dementia. 50 Because of the smaller frequency in the MCI category at the last diagnosis ( n = 72), cognitive status was categorized into a binary variable as normal versus MCI or dementia (MCI/dementia); participants with non-specific ‘impaired’ designation at last clinical diagnosis ( n = 16) were set to missing for this variable. Covariates Covariates included age at death, sex, UK-ADRC follow-up time in years, education in years, APOE genotype, self-reported lifetime medical histories (yes/no) of diabetes, hypertension, myocardial infarction, congestive heart failure, history of coronary artery bypass surgery and history of angioplasty. APOE ɛ4 genotyping APOE genotypes were determined by polymerase chain reaction, as previously described. 51 APOE genotype data was available for 741 participants. The APOE ɛ4 dose effect on the diagnosis of cancer and clinical MCI/dementia diagnosis was assessed in these participants. Statistical analyses All unadjusted comparisons of demographic, neuropathological and clinical variables for participants with and without a diagnosis of cancer were made using χ 2 tests for categorical variables, t -tests and Wilcoxon-Mann-Whitney tests for continuous variables. To examine the association between cancer and outcomes of interest (neuropathological and clinical diagnosis at the visit before death), we used binary and multinomial logistic regression models to estimate the adjusted odds ratios (OR) and 95% confidence intervals (CI). Fourteen dependent variables were evaluated in separate models. Statistical significance was set at 0.05, and the Holm-Bonferroni procedure 52 was used to preserve the family-wise type 1 error rate for multiple comparisons within each set of analysis (14 hypothesis tests in each). All the analyses were conducted in SAS 9.4 (SAS, Inc., Cary, NC). To account for confounders (i.e. shared causes of cancer and neuropathology), we applied stabilized inverse probability of treatment weights (SIPTW) to the logistic models. 53 SIPTW balances the distribution of measured confounding variables in the exposure groups (here cancer diagnosis versus no cancer diagnosis) by creating a ‘pseudo-population’ 53 where the probability of having cancer diagnosis is conditionally independent of the confounding variables. SIPTW were computed by fitting a logistic regression model to the data to estimate the probability of cancer diagnosis conditional on age at death, sex, education and APOE ɛ4 allele dose. To stabilize the weights, the marginal probability of cancer (for those with cancer diagnosis) or no cancer (for those without cancer diagnosis) was multiplied by the inverse conditional probabilities. Because the application of the weights can cause underestimates of parameter variance, we used robust standard errors. 53 Adequacy of covariate balance in the weighted pseudo-population was assessed by examining the distribution of weighted variables graphically and by standardized mean differences. 53 TDP-43 was not recognized as a biomarker of neurodegeneration until 2006, 54 and arteriolosclerosis was not systematically evaluated at UK-ADRC until 2002, yielding substantial missing data for these two pathologies ( Supplementary Table 1 ). In the models evaluating the association between cancer diagnosis and TDP-43 and arteriosclerosis pathologies, we used joint stabilized inverse probability of treatment and censoring weighting. 53 , 55 Stabilized inverse probability of censoring weighting was used under the assumption that data were missing at random. Like SIPTW, stabilized inverse probability of censoring weighting is based on the estimation of the inverse of the probability of the data being observed for each participant in the dataset conditional on their confounders and cancer diagnosis, multiplied by the marginal probability of the data being observed. All observations are used to estimate the censoring weights, with a missingness indicator as the dependent variable. The joint weights are the product of the stabilized inverse probability of censoring weighting and SIPTW. In the resulting pseudo-population, the conditional probability of cancer is independent of the measured confounders and where the conditional probability of being included in the observed data (that is, having TDP-43 and arteriosclerosis data) is independent of the measured confounders and cancer diagnosis. 53 The joint weights were then used in the logistic models where TDP-43 and arteriolosclerosis were the outcomes, again with robust standard errors. The weighted sample was evaluated for robustness as described previously. To evaluate the association between cancer diagnosis and MMSE, the mean scores were estimated at two time points, at ∼6 years and ≤2 years before death. The same weighting procedures described before were used to account for confounding. The weights were then used in the analysis of covariance (ANCOVA) model to estimate the means. To account for the possibility that dementia cases were overrepresented in the UK-ADRC autopsy cohort due to selection bias, we repeated all analyses on a restricted cohort of participants who were recruited into the study with normal cognition ( n = 404). To assess the influence of the timing of cancer diagnoses and cognitive status on the results, we repeated the analyses on a restricted cohort of participants excluding participants with prevalent cancers ( n = 50) and a separate sensitivity analysis was performed on a restricted cohort who were both cancer free and cognitively normal at baseline ( n = 375). The associations of the dose of the APOE ɛ4 alleles and the outcomes of cancer diagnosis and clinical MCI/dementia diagnosis were evaluated using logistic regression with an additive model of inheritance adjusted for covariates age at death and sex. This analysis was limited to the 741 participants with APOE genotype data. Data availability UK-ADRC data and analytical code are available from the authors on reasonable request. Results The final study sample included 785 autopsied participants with detailed clinical and pathological data that was linked to KCR cancer surveillance data ( Fig. 1 ). The data linkage with KCR identified 190 (24.2%) participants with cancer diagnosis. Participant characteristics are presented in Table 1 . The mean ± SD of UK-ADRC follow-up time was 9.2 ± 5.5 years (i.e. study baseline to death), the mean age at study entry was 75.1 ± 8.2, the mean age of death was 83.8 ± 8.6 years and 60.1% of the sample ( n = 472) were females. In genetic analyses, 255 (32.5%) had one APOE ɛ4 allele and 57 (7.3%) had two ɛ4 alleles. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Participant selection flow chart. Table 1. Demographic and clinical characteristics of autopsied UK-ADRC participants by cancer diagnosis Participant characteristics All participants ( n = 785) Cancer diagnosis ( n = 190) No cancer diagnosis ( n = 595) P -value a Age at baseline, years, mean (SD) 75.1 (8.2) 74.7 (7.5) 75.3 (8.3) 0.3449 Age at death, years, mean (SD) 83.8 (8.6) 84.5 (7.8) 83.6 (8.9) 0.1159 Follow-up time, years, mean (SD) 9.2 (5.5) 10.0 (5.9) 8.9 (5.4) 0.0192 Female sex 472 (60.1) 108 (56.8) 364 (61.2) 0.3707 Race (White) 775 (98.7) 183 (96.3) 603 (98.7) 0.0568 Education, years, mean (SD) 15.0 (3.2) 15.8 (3.0) 14.8 (3.2) 0.0002 APOE ɛ4 allele 0.0072 None 429 (54.7) 123 (64.7) 306 (51.4) 1 ɛ4 allele 255 (32.5) 52 (27.4) 203 (34.1) >1 ɛ4 allele 57 (7.3) 10 (5.3) 47 (7.9) Baseline clinical diagnosis <0.0001 Normal 404 (51.5) 144 (75.8) 260 (43.7) MCI/dementia 361 (46.0) 44 (23.2) 317 (53.3) Last clinical diagnosis <0.0001 Normal 227 (31.0) 87 (45.8) 140 (23.5) MCI/dementia 539 (68.7) 95 (50.0) 444 (74.6) Hypertension (present) 429 (54.7) 118 (62.1) 311 (52.3) 0.1671 Diabetes (present) 104 (13.3) 26 (13.7) 78 (13.1) 0.7407 Myocardial Infarction (present) 97 (12.4) 21 (11.1) 76 (12.8) 0.2870 Congestive heart failure (present) 101 (12.9) 25 (13.2) 76 (12.8) 0.7661 Angioplasty (present) 52 (6.6) 14 (7.4) 38 (6.4) 0.8897 Coronary artery bypass surgery 60 (7.6) 14 (7.4) 46 (7.7) 0.6033 Open in a new tab Values are presented as n (%) unless otherwise stated. Missing data are described in Supplementary Table 1 . P -values were obtained from chi-square tests for categorical variables, t -tests and Wilcoxon-Mann-Whitney tests for continuous variables. APOE ɛ4 allele = apolipoprotein ɛ4 allele. a Comparisons are cancer diagnosis versus no cancer diagnosis. Participants with cancer diagnosis had more years of education (15.8 versus 14.8 years, P < 0.001) and a lower average dosage of the APOE ɛ4 allele ( P = 0.0072) compared to cancer-free participants. Participants with cancer diagnosis had a higher prevalence (45.8% versus 23.5%) of cognitively normal diagnosis at the last UK-ADRC assessment, whereas MCI/dementia (50.0%) was less prevalent in the participants with cancer diagnosis versus among the cancer-free participants (74.6%), P < 0.0001. Among the participants who were cognitively normal at baseline ( n = 404) ( Supplementary Table 2 ), participants with cancer diagnosis died younger (mean ± SD), 85.3 ± 7.4 years compared to the cancer-free participants 87.9 ± 2.2, P < 0.001. In this group, sex, educational attainment, APOE ɛ4 alleles and last visit clinical diagnosis were similar among those with and without cancer. Cancer characteristics by cognitive diagnosis are shown in Table 2 . At baseline, 50 (26.3%) participants had a diagnosis of cancer preceding enrolment in the UK-ADRC, while 83 (43.7%) were diagnosed during UK-ADRC follow-up and 57 (30.0%) were diagnosed after the last visit. Breast cancer was diagnosed in 33 (17.4%) participants, gastrointestinal cancers in 32 (16.8%) and lung cancer in 29 (15.2%), while other types of cancer were less common. Participants were most often diagnosed with localized stage cancer (42.1%). Most (60.0%) cancer cases were treated with a combination of treatments such as surgery and radiation and with/without other therapies, but not including chemotherapy. Most cancers were non-smoking-related (57.4%). Table 2. Distribution of cancer case characteristics by final cognitive status Cancer case characteristics All cancer cases ( n = 190) Normal status a ( n = 87) MCI/dementia status a ( n = 95) Cancer diagnosis in relation to ADRC enrolment period D