ages (III/IV and V/VI), moderate/frequent diffuse and neuritic plaques, Lewy bodies, moderate/severe cerebral atrophy and moderate/severe cerebral amyloid angiopathy. By contrast, TDP-43, hippocampal sclerosis and cerebrovascular pathologies were similar in distribution between the two groups. Among participants who were cognitively normal at baseline ( n = 404), cancer diagnosis was significantly associated with lower prevalence of higher Braak NFT stages (III/IV and V/VI), moderate/severe diffuse plaques and presence of infarcts/lacunes ( Supplementary Table 3 ). Table 3. Neuropathological characteristics of autopsied UK-ADRC participants by cancer diagnosis ( n = 785) Neuropathology characteristics All participants ( n = 785) Cancer diagnosis ( n = 190) No cancer diagnosis ( n = 595) P -value Brain weight, g, mean ± SD 1158.8 ± 154.2 1187.6 ± 163.9 1149.9 ± 150.1 0.002 Braak NFT stage <0.0001 0/I/II 247 (31.5) 95 (50.3) 152 (26.3) III/IV 133 (16.9) 33 (17.5) 100 (17.3) V/VI 387 (49.3) 61 (32.3) 326 (56.4) Diffuse plaques <0.0001 None/sparse 170 (21.7) 63 (33.2) 107 (18.0) Moderate/frequent 614 (66.8) 127 (66.8) 448 (82.0) Neuritic plaques <0.0001 None/sparse 255 (32.5) 90 (47.4) 165 (27.8) Moderate/frequent 529 (67.5) 100 (52.6) 316 (72.2) Lewy bodies (present) 264 (33.9) 51 (26.8) 213 (35.9) 0.0294 TDP-43 (present) 165 (20.0) 39 (20.5) 126 (20.3) 0.1267 Cerebral atrophy 0.0038 None/mild 521 (67.2) 144 (75.8) 377 (63.4) Moderate/severe 254 (32.8) 46 (24.2) 208 (35.0) Hippocampal sclerosis (present) 133 (16.9) 27 (14.2) 106 (17.8) 0.2724 Cerebral amyloid angiopathy 0.0011 None/mild 553 (70.5) 153 (80.5) 400 (67.4) Moderate/severe 214 (27.3) 35 (18.4) 179 (29.8) Atherosclerosis 0.9757 <50% Occluded 370 (47.1) 91 (47.9) 279 (46.9) ≥50% Occluded 400 (51.0) 98 (51.6) 302 (50.8) Arteriolosclerosis 0.4476 None/mild 495 (63.1) 131 (69.0) 364 (61.2) Moderate/severe 145 (18.5) 43 (22.6) 102 (17.1) Infarcts/lacunes (present) 348 (44.3) 78 (41.1) 270 (45.4) 0.2961 Argyrophilic grain disease (present) 113 (15.0) 25 (13.2) 88 (14.8) 0.5292 Open in a new tab Values are presented as n (%) unless stated otherwise. Missing data are reported in Supplementary Table 1 . P -values are for results of chi-square tests for categorical variables, comparing cancer versus no cancer. In SIPTW multivariable logistic regression models, diagnosis of cancer was associated with ∼55% lower odds (OR = 0.45; 95% CI 0.32–0.65; P < 0.0001) (data not shown) of MCI/dementia. Participants with cancer diagnosis had an estimated 62% decreased odds (OR = 0.38; 95% CI 0.26–0.55; P < 0.0001) of having Braak NFT V/VI stages and 48% decreased odds (OR = 0.52; 95% CI 0.37–0.86; P = 0.0147) of Braak NFT III/IV stages versus Braak NFT 0/I/II stages ( Table 4 ). Cancer diagnosis was associated with lower odds of moderate/frequent diffuse (OR = 0.52; 95% CI 0.35–0.78; P = 0.0013), neuritic plaques (OR = 0.52; 95% CI 0.36–0.75; P = 0.0005) and cerebral amyloid angiopathy (OR = 0.58; 95% CI 0.38–0.91; P = 0.0183). However, the statistical significance of the results for cerebral amyloid angiopathy and Braak NFT stage III/IV did not survive the Holm-Bonferroni correction. For analyses restricted to incident cancers, the results were similar to the results obtained that included prevalent cancers ( Table 4 ). Table 4. Weighted odds ratios for neuropathological features (cancer diagnosis versus no cancer diagnosis) Dependent variables Weighted OR (95% CI) All participants ( n = 785) Cancer free at baseline ( n = 735) Cognitively normal at baseline ( n = 404) Cognitively normal and cancer free at baseline ( n = 375) Braak NFT stage III/IV versus 0/I/II 0.52 ( 0.37–0.86) * 0.49 ( 0.28–0.84) * 0.55 ( 0.32–0.95) * 0.55 ( 0.31–0.98) * V/VI versus 0/I/II 0.38 ( 0.26–0.55) 0.32 ( 0.20–0.53) 0.79 (0.46–1.36) 0.70 (0.39–1.26) Diffuse plaques Moderate/frequent versus none/sparse 0.52 ( 0.35–0.78) 0.47 ( 0.31–0.73) 0.67 (0.41–1.06) 0.67 (0.42–1.08) Neuritic plaques Moderate/frequent versus no