.0017) and ≤2 years (difference of 4.1 points, P < 0.0001) prior to death ( Supplementary Table 4 ). Among the participants who were cognitively normal at baseline, the difference of weighted mean MMSE scores was only significantly higher among the cancer diagnosis participants ≤2 years prior to death (difference of 1.6 points, P = 0.0353; Supplementary Table 4 ). Finally, the associations were examined between the dosage of APOE ɛ4 alleles and the diagnosis of cancer and the cognitive diagnosis. Increasing dosage of APOE ɛ4 allele was associated with lower odds of cancer diagnosis (OR = 0.69; 95% CI = 0.52–0.93; P = 0.0127) and higher odds of MCI/dementia (OR = 2.92; 95% CI 2.12–4.02; P < 0.0001) (data not shown). Discussion The current study, based on epidemiological surveillance cancer registry data with neuropathological evaluation in all participants, supported the hypotheses that cancer diagnosis is associated with lower odds of all-cause dementia diagnosis, as well as a reduced burden of Alzheimer’s disease pathology. We examined the association of cancer diagnosis with multiple outcomes that have not been extensively evaluated in previous research. Alzheimer’s disease pathology, as well as cerebral amyloid angiopathy, were lower in all participants with a cancer diagnosis, including incident cancers. Examination of MMSE test score data suggested that participants with cancer diagnosis had better cognition compared to cancer-free participants before death. The inverse relationship between cancer and dementia has generated interest among researchers and has been reported in multiple studies. 5–8 , 10 , 12 , 14 , 18 , 20 , 22 , 24 However, only two previous studies have reported on the association of cancer and neuropathology. 23 , 24 In the current study, cancer diagnosis was associated with lower odds of Alzheimer’s disease neuropathology (NFTs, and both neuritic and diffuse amyloid plaques), higher MMSE, Animal Naming scores and a lower percentage with clinical dementia. By contrast, we did not find consistent and significant associations between cancer diagnosis with the presence of Lewy bodies, TDP-43 or cerebrovascular pathologies. Most previous reports operationalized Alzheimer’s disease or dementia according to clinical, rather than pathological, parameters. A recent multicentre cohort study found that among individuals with either mild or isolated cognitive complaints, incident cancer was associated with a reduced risk (∼50%) of dementia, accounting for various biases. 29 A retrospective cohort of 3.5 million elderly veterans, survivors of most cancers had a reduced risk of Alzheimer’s disease, but increased risk of the alternative outcomes (non-Alzheimer’s disease dementia, osteoarthritis, stroke and macular degeneration). 11 Using data from the Framingham Heart Study, Driver et al . 7 reported that the protective effect of previous cancer was greater for smoking-related cancers than for non-smoking-related cancers, accounting for competing risk of mortality. Ording et al . 15 used a nationwide Danish cohort that included patients with dementia and Parkinson’s disease, and reported lower standardized incidence rate ratios and absolute reduction cancer risk during 10 years follow-up for Alzheimer’s disease, vascular dementia and all-cause dementia. However, in another study, Ording et al . 22 analysed individuals surviving cancer >10 years and reported that the standardized incidence rate ratios for incident diagnoses of Alzheimer’s disease, after stratification by sex, age and cancer stage, approached that of the general population. Similarly, Hanson et al . 56 report that modelling cancer as a time-varying predictor and accounting for the competing risk of death before dementia attenuates the inverse relationship between cancer and Alzheimer’s disease, although even here the association persisted for males aged 70 and older, and females aged 75 and older. A recently published study using Mendelian randomization to assess the causal association between cancer (data from genome-wide association studies) and Alzheimer’s disease [summary statistics from the International Genomics of Alzheimer’s Project (IGAP)], found that genetically predicted cancer (lung, leukaemia, breast) and smoking-related cancers were associated with lower odds of clinically diagnosed Alzheimer’s disease. 16 This study results provide additional evidence for the inverse association of cancer diagnosis and Alzheimer’s disease pathologies. Both of these clinical syndromes are characterized by a set of molecular determinants (e.g. growth factors, inflammatory mechanisms and cytoskeletal pathways) that are either complementarily deregulated or share remarkably overlapping functional pathways. 57 To provide one example, the PIN 1 gene is overexpressed in certain cancers but is downregulated in Alzheimer’s disease pathogenesis and neuronal degeneration. 58–60 Experimental cellular models demonstrated that PIN 1 knockout increases amyloid-β 42 production, suggesting that it might favour amyloidogenic amyloid precursor protein processing and elevate amyloid-β 42 in an age-dependent manner. 61 Transcription factor p53 (tumour suppressor) is reported to be deleted or dysregulated in many cancers but upregulated in neurodegenerative diseases. 57 , 62 APOE ɛ4 is a strong risk factor for late-onset Alzheimer’s disease, 27 , 28 and in the current study we found a negative association between APOE ɛ4 and cancer diagnosis. Although there was a higher prevalence of the APOE ɛ4 allele among the cancer-free participants versus those with cancer diagnosis, this difference was not observed among the participants who were cognitively normal at baseline. We were not able to comprehensively test the associations of all three common APOE alleles ( ɛ 2, ɛ 3 and ɛ4 ) due to small cell sizes. 28 We note that there are differing results in the literature concerning a possible role of APOE in cancer risk—in humans and animal models. 63 , 64 Thus, further studies are required to better understand whether APOE is associated with cancer risk. As a practical point, the present study shows the importance of genetic characterization and controlling for APOE status. The inverse association between cancer and Alzheimer’s disease-type dementia could be potentially explained by many factors and potential confounders. For example, a cancer diagnosis may also bring about behavioural changes in persons at risk for, or having survived, cancer. 24 Furthermore, cancer may be less often be screened and diagnosed in cognitively impaired individuals. 22 The role of survivor bias cannot be ruled out completely; however, a recently published simulation study demonstrated that selective survival was too small to explain the observed inverse cancer-dementia link, suggesting other mechanisms drive this association. 21 In the current study, the average age at death of the participants was >83 years and was similar among those with and without a cancer diagnosis. It has also been reported that exposure to oncologic chemotherapeutic drugs is associated with lower Alzheimer’s disease risk than radiation therapy. 11 A large study using a population-based cancer registry found that chemotherapy decreased the risk of Alzheimer’s disease death in white females diagnosed with breast cancer at the age of 65 or older. 13 Chemotherapeutic drugs such as taxanes are microtubule stabilizers, have been investigated for their role in reducing tau pathology as a treatment for Alzheimer’s disease and related tauopathies. 65 Some studies have reported cancer chemotherapy treatments to decrease cognitive functions in cancer survivors, 13 , 30 , 66 an effect referred to as ‘chemo brain’. In the current study, only 15% of the participants with cancer diagnosis received chemotherapy; we did not examine associations with cancer treatment subtypes, due to statistical considerations regarding sparse data. A major strength of the study is that we linked the UK-ADRC data to the KCR, so the cancer cases were confirmed by pathologists, and not self-reported. The KCR is a SEER population-based registry, so it is relatively unlikely that the cancer diagnoses were missed or inappropriately diagnosed. However, cases diagnosed before 1995 could have been missed. The longitudinal cohort at the UK-ADRC complemented the cancer registry data with rich cognitive and neuropathological data. Additionally, the availability of APOE genotype status, which has not been commonly reported by previous research studies, was critical to adjust and evaluate cancer and dementia associations. 10 , 14 , 22 Previous research studies have not included multiple aspects of evaluation i.e. clinical diagnosis, longitudinal cognitive scores, genetic and neuropathological evaluations in reporting the association of cancer and dementia. Furthermore, we used inverse probability weighting to balance the measured confounding variables and to address missing data. The study had some limitations. Because of the relatively low number of participants with cancer ( n = 190), we could not investigate associations relevant to site-specific cancers, the treatments or the stages of cancer. Furthermore, our study included only participants who died at age ≥60 years, and the participants were primarily white and well-educated, which limits generalizability of the findings. The sample size also limited our ability to assess associations with other participant characteristics, such as medical history and cancer treatment. In conclusion, this innovative study used an epidemiological SEER cancer surveillance registry with pathologically confirmed cancer cases (and controls lacking cancer diagnosis), for comparison in the context of a community-based autopsy cohort of elderly volunteers with longitudinal neurocognitive testing and matched neuropathological evaluation. We observed persistent inverse association between cancer and dementia in our study, and this study adds neuropathological evidence to the existing literature on epidemiological cohorts and clinical dementia. Although our findings provide strong new data in favour of the inverse association with Alzheimer’s disease-type pathology, the underlying mechanisms remain to be explained. Supplementary Material awac035_Supplementary_Data Click here for additional data file. (140.5KB, pdf) Acknowledgements The authors would like to thank the participants and their families, as well as the faculty and staff at the University of Kentucky Alzheimer’s Disease Research Center and Kentucky Cancer Registry for providing cancer data. Abbreviations KCR Kentucky Cancer Registry MCI mild cognitive impairment MMSE Mini-Mental State Examination NFT neurofibrillary tangle SIPTW stabilized inverse probability of treatment weights TDP-43 transactive response DNA-binding protein 43 kDa UK-ADRC University of Kentucky Alzheimer’s Disease Research Center Contributor Information Shama D Karanth, Aging and Geriatric Research, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL 32610, USA; Cancer Control and Population Sciences Program, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL 32610, USA. Yuriko Katsumata, Sanders-Brown Center on Aging, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY 40536, USA; Department of Biostatistics, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY 40536, USA. Peter T Nelson, Sanders-Brown Center on Aging, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY 40536, USA; Department of Pathology, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY 40536, USA. David W Fardo, Sanders-Brown Center on Aging, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY 40536, USA; Department of Biostatistics, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY 40536, USA. Jaclyn K McDowell, Department of Epidemiology, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY 40536, USA; Markey Cancer Control Program, Kentucky Cancer Registry, Lexington, KY 40504, USA. Frederick A Schmitt, Sanders-Brown Center on Aging, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY 40536, USA; Department of Neurology