neurodegenerative diseases. Trends Mol Med. 2017;23:512–533. doi: 10.1016/j.molmed.2017.03.008. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 93. Zhao Y, Wu X, Li X, Jiang LL, Gui X, Liu Y, Sun Y, Zhu B, Piña-Crespo JC, Zhang M, Zhang N, Chen X, Bu G, An Z, Huang TY, Xu H. TREM2 Is a receptor for β-amyloid that mediates microglial function. Neuron. 2018;97:1023–1031.e7. doi: 10.1016/j.neuron.2018.01.031. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Additional file 1: Table S1. (57.7KB, xlsx) Additional file 2: Table S2. (13.1KB, xlsx) Additional file 3: Table S3. (70KB, xlsx) Additional file 4: Figure S1–S21. (12.8MB, docx)',39,'2025-10-15 11:05:44','2025-10-15 11:05:44');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5262,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12344323','A Qualitative Examination of Job Satisfaction in Emergency Medicine From the Perspective of Physicians Also Working in Prehospital and Retrieval Medicine.','Myers JA, Aldington S, Powell DMC.','2025','helicopter preconceived otago d23 eth00779 relentlessness resuscitating welcoming intoxicated undervalued relentless downtime chaotic insurmountable chill crazily broke thinly enjoying resuscitating sickest tempered overburdened rostering blessing juggle downside fatiguing hate swim midweek discontent restructure 50s fatigued slept whatsoever backing welcoming knit appreciative bane phoning tiring expletive phew undervalued undervalued upskilling unsustainable upskill invigorate triathlete premium grads adventure grind helicopter jmo ringing sorry destroying swathes amazing shit dissatisfying flyers rude helicopter hesitate fatigued patriarchal synchronised discard grinding tenfold invigorate expletive wanna tasman bloods unprofessional creep overburdened sickest upskilling encumbered generalisable generously otago otago aldington e70121 flowerdew wynne waddington mckenney elkbuli aotearoa stehman wegman e34982 darbyshire isba basit petravic bajec tiefengraber slavec strnad swancott workforces e087485 benger gerrard vears gillam hazell elahi abid incivility forgiveness sancton sloss strydom mccracken e354 e360 korczak yakubu angell e21462 pek getmedia 50c16051 41e9 4a44 b9a9 badecc82229c care_final','ABSTRACT Objective Low job satisfaction amongst emergency physicians is a significant risk to career sustainability. Anecdotal evidence suggests that prehospital and retrieval medicine (PHARM) physicians experience relatively high job satisfaction. The objective of this qualitative study was to explore the research question: ‘What influences job satisfaction and sustainability for emergency physicians who maintain dual roles in PHARM and the emergency department?’ Methods Eligible emergency physicians from Australia and New Zealand were interviewed in‐depth about their experiences and perceptions of job satisfaction and sustainability. The research team applied experiential knowledge and an inductive analytic approach to construct a conceptual understanding of the physicians'' job satisfaction from the data, including factors contributing to career sustainability. Results Our sample included 11 participants, six from Australia and five from New Zealand. We identified six key themes influencing job satisfaction: workload, job factors, personal factors, focus on well‐being, culture and management and bureaucracy. These are explored in detail, along with ideas on career sustainability. Conclusions The nature of emergency medicine work remains inherently satisfying for participants in this study but is influenced by the culture and current working conditions found in many emergency departments, particularly when these factors impact the standard of clinical care physicians feel they can provide. Our findings offer practical guidance on protective factors for job satisfaction and sustainability, including fractional appointments, diversity and flexibility of practice, continuous professional development and access to professional supervision or peer support. Preserving career longevity in emergency medicine requires the implementation of evidence‐based, sustainable initiatives. 1. Introduction Recommendations published almost a decade ago identified emergency medicine (EM) workforce retention and job satisfaction as a ‘top 10’ research priority for the speciality [ 1 ]. This remains imperative, with workloads and conditions continuing to pose significant risks to career sustainability [ 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 ]. Studies have linked EM workforce attrition to many factors, including job satisfaction, workplace culture, peer support, training, education, work‐life balance and burnout [ 7 , 8 , 9 , 10 ]. Few studies have focused on understanding these complex issues by examining the experiences and perspectives of EM consultants in depth [ 11 , 12 ]. In particular, there has been little focus on developing a conceptual understanding of the factors that influence job satisfaction and EM workforce retention [ 8 ]. While many Australasian emergency physicians work primarily in hospital‐based emergency departments (EDs), some also work a proportion of their shifts for helicopter‐based EM services as prehospital and retrieval medicine (PHARM) physicians [ 13 ]. Although not extensively studied, anecdotal evidence suggests that low attrition rates amongst PHARM physicians indicate relatively high job satisfaction. The objective of this research was to explore job satisfaction in EM from the perspective of PHARM physicians, answering the research question: ‘What influences job satisfaction and sustainability for emergency physicians who maintain dual roles in PHARM and the emergency department?’. We aimed to generate knowledge that could be practically applied in supporting job satisfaction and role sustainability in EDs. 2. Methods The focus of this study was on understanding emergency physicians'' personal experiences and perspectives on what influences their own job satisfaction and sustainability. We therefore employed an inductive qualitative methodology to enable us to identify patterns and derive key themes directly from the data, rather than imposing preconceived categories or constructs. Between May 2023 and April 2024, Australasian EM physicians working in dual PHARM and ED roles were interviewed in depth. All interviews were audio‐recorded, transcribed and then de‐identified. The focus of questioning included learning from differences they experience between the two settings and their suggestions for promoting role sustainability. Ethical approval was obtained from the University of Otago Human Ethics Committee (ref: D23/014) and the Sydney Local Health District Ethics Review Committee (ref: 2023/ ETH00779 ); all participants provided informed consent prior to being interviewed. The data were analysed by J.A.M. and S.A. To generate findings with direct relevance for health professional practice and policy, we applied experiential knowledge and inductive content analysis (ICA) to construct a conceptual framework of influences on job satisfaction from the data, including factors that contribute to career sustainability [ 14 ]. As recommended for ICA, we generated codes directly from the data, following an iterative process that refined initial ‘big picture’ codes into a more detailed coding schema. From this, we then developed the key themes relevant to our research question [ 14 ]. 3. Results 3.1. Participant Characteristics Table 1 presents the characteristics of the 11 physicians who were enrolled in the study. Participants ranged in age and experience and, on average, worked two ED‐based and one PHARM‐based shift per week. TABLE 1. Participant characteristics ( N = 11). Age Mean 46 years (SD 7); range 35–56 years Gender 4 female; 7 male Current location of practice 5 in New Zealand; 6 in Australia Country of training 5 Australia; 4 New Zealand; 1 Canada; 1 United Kingdom No. of years working as an EM consultant Mean 13 years (SD 7); range 4–24 years No. of years working in PHARM Mean 11 years (SD 7); range 3–25 years ED‐based shifts per week Median 2 (IQR 1.5–3); range 1–3 PHARM‐based shifts per week Median 1 (IQR 1–2 shifts); range 1–3 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: ED, emergency department; IQR, interquartile range; PHARM, prehospital and retrieval medicine; SD, standard deviation. Based on our conceptual framework of interacting influences on physicians'' job satisfaction, we categorised our findings into six main themes: workload, job factors, personal factors, focus on well‐being, culture and management and bureaucracy. These themes offer a framework for understanding the dissatisfaction and burnout experienced by many emergency physicians, while also identifying positive protective factors and strategies for achieving a sustainable career in EM (Table 2 ). TABLE 2. Factors affecting job satisfaction in emergency departments. Positive factors Negative factors Seen as a privilege Relentlessness Positive interactions with patients Interruptions and competing priorities Resuscitating critically unwell patients Unable to perform the job to a high standard Variety and case mix Long patient waiting times Breadth of skills Non‐acute patients Welcoming and diverse speciality Futility in managing elderly patients without advanced care directives Teamwork ‘Difficult patients’—violent, intoxicated Work is not ‘taken home’ Evening and night shifts No focus on wellbeing Limited leave Interdepartmental conflict Management focuses on targets that departments are unable to achieve Poor remuneration compared to other specialities Undervalued by management Open in a new tab 3.2. Workload Workload was a dominant theme, with many participants describing ED shifts to be ‘relentless’, where there is no opportunity to take proper breaks and physicians feel under too much time pressure to do their job properly. In contrast, prehospital medicine often affords some downtime between missions, and the norm is having one patient at a time. …everything''s chaotic. No one''s got any time to sort of try and fix things. The problems seem insurmountable. …emergency medicine people do retrieval to have a bit of a chill out, it''s easily the less stressful job. You have moments of stress, but you''re not in a heightened state for 12 hours. Participants also raised concerns about the clinical risk to patients resulting from workload pressures and their inability to focus on patients due to competing priorities and constant interruptions. Not being able to perform the role to an expected standard is a particular source of job dissatisfaction, while patient waiting times are a source of significant moral injury. …it was so crazily busy that it was unsafe. And you just broke yourself and you didn''t do particularly great medicine because you were just spread so thinly. 3.3. Job Factors Consideration of job factors was perhaps the most revealing in terms of what brings emergency physicians the most satisfaction, with most participants finding the job a privilege and enjoying the humanitarian aspects of the role. Resuscitating critically ill or injured patients is a potent driver of job satisfaction; perhaps more evident in PHARM, where the very nature of the job involves caring for the sickest patients. I mean, it''s a privileged job to do. You''re in a very special place where you get to save people''s lives, you get to see people having the worst days of their lives, you get to make a difference. This rewarding aspect of the job appears to be tempered by an increasing burden of non‐critical patients, some of whom are seeking care because their needs are not being met in an increasingly overburdened primary care system. A related concern was specific to the ageing population, often delivered to ED without prior consideration of end‐of‐life wishes. We treat old people…. with no quality of life, referred in after a fall with a bang on the head and it''s really clear from the get‐go that, even if there was a bleed in their head that they wouldn''t be for neurosurgery. But once they are in, it''s very hard to get them back—and often the family always want everything done. Interactions with patients were largely viewed as a positive factor, though some patients are considered ‘difficult’ for a variety of non‐clinical reasons (e.g., aggression), which results in a far less satisfying interaction. I love patients. The patient contact component is probably the most enjoyable part of it for me. Non‐patient‐centred job factors included shift work, rostering and remuneration. Shift work appears to be a mixed blessing. For some, shift patterns offer a more flexible lifestyle and an ability to juggle family and extracurricular life. The lack of on‐call commitments and the ability to take uninterrupted time off were also viewed as positives. The downside is the physical and mental toll of evening and night shifts, which are intrinsically more fatiguing. Some people hate shift work but I quite like shift work and what it gives me. I mean I''m off today and everyone else is at work. And if I''ve got an afternoon shift then I get a morning that I can go for a swim or a run, meet someone for lunch. Like I think having that time off midweek is really important. Remuneration was a source of discontent; especially compared to other specialists who often have the option to work in private practice. I guess we get paid very well on the population scale, but when you compare to other specialities, who have done the same amount of training as us, I don''t think we get paid very well, and so it does mean you''re obliged to work until retirement essentially. And there''s no scope for private practice that could give other people options to sort of back off their hours or restructure their work as they age. 3.4. Personal Factors Participants varied in age from 35 to 56, and some older participants raised concerns about the impact of ageing on both performance and resilience to the demands of the job, particularly in PHARM, which relies more on physical fitness. There was also concern that age was associated with a reduced resilience to the demands of shift work, particularly night shifts, which require longer periods of recovery. There''s some consideration that we need to get into, about how we manage late career specialists. So, not having over‐50s work night shifts in the emergency department—I think that''s reasonable. Or you might want to step down from the resuscitation area. 3.5. Focus on Well‐Being Participants universally drew attention to the significantly more proactive stance on well‐being in the PHARM setting, particularly regarding fatigue, where robust measures are in place to monitor fatigue and a culture exists that acknowledges both the propensity to become fatigued and its impact on both patient safety and physician well‐being. This contrasts with the hospital environment, where there is considerable discussion about well‐being, but few tangible initiatives are implemented. In some EDs, fatigue and burnout are exacerbated by the limited availability of leave due to inadequate staffing. There''s a real focus on fatigue management [in retrieval] … and whether you''ve slept well recently. There''s rules on how much you can work, how many hours you can fly in a shift … whereas in the hospital, there''s a lot of noise made about self‐care, and well‐being, and positivity and everything, but there''s just no delivery whatsoever. Nothing backing it up. 3.6. Culture EM is seen as a diverse, progressive and welcoming speciality, perhaps due to its relative youth compared to other more established specialities. The most overwhelmingly positive cultural aspect described was effective teamwork, which, particularly in PHARM, was recognised as a protective factor. It''s a really tight‐knit little team, and it''s just we''ve all got the same goals, and that''s not necessarily the case in the emergency department where there''s a lot of competing interests at times. However, hospital‐based interactions with wider clinical teams, such as inpatient speciality colleagues, are largely experienced as negative due to the direction of the referral. It contrasts with PHARM, where interactions with other teams and hospital departments are much more respectful and appreciative. You''re basically like the bane of everyone''s life in the hospital. Every time you''re phoning someone… It''s like you''re putting something onto someone else. That''s tiring, to be that person who gets spoken to like [expletive] by the rest of the hospital. [In retrieval] You get so much respect from people from everywhere you turn up. You arrive at a hospital and they''re like ‘oh thank God you''re here’, or you come to a scene and they''re like, ‘phew thank God’. 3.7. Management and Bureaucracy Interactions with management and the inability to effect positive change due to bureaucratic processes were key contributors to job dissatisfaction. Management''s perception of the worth of emergency physicians led to participants feeling undervalued. This contrasts with their perceived high value in retrieval. I think there''s a real feeling within emergency medicine of being undervalued. I feel like I''m empowered in my pre‐hospital job. I feel like I''m listened to. I feel like I''m valued more. 3.8. Sustainability and Longevity Participants were also asked to consider factors they considered important for career sustainability and longevity. The identified ideas included fractional ED appointments, diversity of practice, continuous learning and upskilling and the need for professional supervision or peer support. Nevertheless, not all participants felt that these factors were sufficient to preserve career longevity. It was always a job that I thought I''d do till I retire. But I don''t know if I can anymore. Most participants characterised full‐time work in an ED as unsustainable. Fractional ED appointments to allow for career diversification were seen as one of the most important factors in ensuring career longevity, with many opportunities available to emergency physicians outside of hospital departments. Taking opportunities to upskill in a specialised area was also seen as a way to invigorate and motivate, and professional support, in the form of supervision or peer support, was considered protective. My advice to people is always that full‐time emergency medicine is not sustainable. So find something else that you can do so that you don''t have to work full‐time in an emergency department. It might just be working less than full‐time and being a triathlete. It might be working as a toxicologist part‐time; it could be all sorts of things. The ability to self‐roster was identified as an important positive factor for multiple reasons. It enables people to better manage smaller fractions across multiple jobs, thereby facilitating role diversification. It also allows people to build in a longer recovery time after an evening shift or a night shift, reducing fatigue. People can manage competing needs of work and family more proactively. Finally, the ability to cluster shifts can facilitate time off at a time when annual leave is at a premium. Participants were asked to comment on how they saw the future of EM and spoke of concerns that the perception amongst junior doctors is that EM is not seen as a viable option. We''re now finding that new grads will come and spend a little bit of time in the emergency department—and quickly run away from it. Interestingly, however, many indicated they would still recommend EM as a speciality, despite the problems encountered. I''d still recommend it as a speciality. I mean, it''s flexible. If you work in a good department, there''s a great team environment. And you can almost choose your own adventure. A summary of illustrative quotes categorised by theme is presented in Table 3 . TABLE 3. Themes and illustrative quotes—summary. Theme Illustrative quotes Workload ‘So the ED can sometimes be a really hard grind like it''s just constant for 10 h to start your shift and you are just constantly going’. ‘You are just fighting fires all day long trying not to let people die’. ‘You don''t have that on the helicopter. You''ve generally got the one patient you can look after. And you can devote all your attention to that patient’. ‘In emergency medicine you can be in the middle of a major trauma resuscitation and a nurse will come and talk to you about the ECG that they want you to sign. Or a JMO''s hanging on to one side wanting to speak to you. Or the phones ringing because there''s a GP with an admitting call or something like that and so you know, that inevitably all of those competing priorities, just add to your level of stress’. ‘I find the department overcrowding and the waiting times very stressful. I''m seeing these people that have waited 6, 8, 10 h, and I am like “I am SO sorry this is not right”, and having to do that over and over and over again… can sometimes get quite soul‐destroying’. ‘There are huge swathes of people in the medical profession who, when the sun isn''t warming the earth think that patients are someone else''s problem. Namely, ours’. Job Factors ‘When a job''s amazing at retrieval. It''s exactly the kind of job you want in emergency. So like retrieval is basically the best bit of emergency without all the other shit’. ‘I think a lot of people feel a little bit lost now and especially when they''ve brought in some of the fees to GPs, some people don''t really know where else to go, or they haven''t had the best care in the community so they end up with us’. ‘I always wanted to be the kind of doctor that could do a huge range of things’. ‘Obviously there''s the difficult patients that can be quite dissatisfying in the ED, so those you know, frequent flyers, psychiatric patients, or just people that can be rude—that can be challenging’. ‘When I''m on leave, I''m on leave. There''s nothing I can do when I''m not actually there, whether that''s pre‐hospital or in the department’. ‘I find working an evening shift much harder than working a day shift both because of the physiology of it, you know, it''s just harder to work through the evening and it''s harder to recover the following day’. Personal factors ‘I have colleagues my age and younger who are talking about stopping helicopter work because of physical health, as they are getting older and their knees are not working. It is more physically demanding than working in a hospital’. Focus on well‐being ‘We''re so short now that it''s just impossible to get leave. So we''ve had several people quit just because they can''t get leave. Essentially, you''ve got to book it a year out’. ‘I think it''s I think it''s more the culture—of understanding, fatigue and looking out for people''s fatigue. I mean nobody would hesitate at putting up their hand and saying, I''m fatigued. I''m not safe’. Culture ‘There''s always been quite good gender diversity in emergency medicine. So there''s none of the patriarchal culture that has, I think, persisted through some of the other specialities’. ‘People are so well trained and synchronised, you know, that everyone''s at the top of their game’. Management and bureaucracy ‘Management just sees us as things to use up and then discard’. ‘It seems like the red tape and the gear grinding that you need to go through to get stuff done is actually multiplied tenfold at the moment’. Sustainability and longevity ‘But what I''ve never seen before is that we''ve got so many young consultants … and they''re looking at exit strategies … that just shouldn''t happen, if the job''s right’. ‘I think anything you do where you initiate change and start doing something new, and learning, that''s going to invigorate you and prolong your career, I think’. ‘The trainees turn up to work and they go [expletive], I don''t wanna do this for the rest of my life’. ‘I think it''s a great gateway to be able to do a lot of different things. Pre‐hospital is certainly not the only thing. But it does open some doors for a lot of different ways to work’. Open in a new tab 4. Discussion This trans‐Tasman study sought to understand influences on job satisfaction and career sustainability from the perspective of emergency physicians who also work in prehospital and retrieval medicine. A qualitative methodology was chosen over a quantitative approach to allow an in‐depth exploration of the issues based on clinicians'' own experience. We found that while the practice of EM may remain inherently satisfying for clinicians, factors outside their control often have a significant negative impact. Our findings align with UK‐based research, which found that emergency physicians'' positive feelings about their clinical work were undermined by psychological pressures and organisational factors they did not have the opportunity to address; which in turn negatively impacted their work, social relationships and personal well‐being [ 11 ]. Our study enabled us to develop a conceptual framework of how factors can interact to influence job satisfaction. Workload, exacerbated by access block, emerged as a powerful negative influence. Competing priorities and multiple interruptions, even during critical procedures and resuscitation, led to a feeling of not being able to do the job well, threatening physicians'' feelings of competence. While most ED physicians would advocate for the need for more staff, previous reviews of optimal staffing models suggest that it is not just total staffing numbers that are important, but also seniority. Having a more senior physician base has been found to reduce waiting times, the number of patients who do not wait for treatment, access block and length of stay [ 15 ]. The constant interruptions that emergency physicians experience contrast with the retrieval physician''s experience of being able to focus on one patient at a time. While this model is clearly not achievable in an ED, there are strategies that might reduce unnecessary interruptions; examples include increasing the threshold for emergency physicians needing to sign ECGs immediately, having a jobs list functionality built into EMR, or having a consultant or senior trainee assigned to triage to initiate X‐rays and order bloods at the time of presentation. The effects of shift work are much more noticeable in the ED than in PHARM, where there is a genuine organisational attempt to mitigate fatigue. The physical toll that shift work takes becomes more apparent with age, with the older participants stating that this was a significant concern for them. A proactive approach, such as removing night shift work for physicians over the age of 55, may increase career longevity. Teamwork was a positive factor in both departmental EM and PHARM, but there is significant tension and conflict for emergency physicians when dealing with inpatient teams and other specialities. There has been a recent focus on civility in the workplace and its role in job satisfaction [ 16 ], and a reduced tolerance for unprofessional behaviour would undoubtedly contribute to an improved overall culture. The creep of low acuity patients into EDs, as a result of overburdened and costly primary health care services, can also be a source of frustration in EDs. Compounding this is the increasing volume of elderly patients whose end‐of‐life wishes are not being adequately addressed in the community. PHARM, by its very nature, focuses on the sickest and most critically unwell patients, which is more aligned with what clinicians expect from a critical care speciality. Research has shown that low acuity patients rarely seek alternative healthcare before presenting to the ED [ 17 ]. Understanding the barriers to this might inform educational strategies to encourage more appropriate use of community resources [ 18 ]. While it was not the focus of this study, our findings also provide some insight into the high rate of burnout amongst emergency physicians. Burnout amongst healthcare workers has been well documented, and emergency physicians are particularly vulnerable, with reported rates approaching 65%–70% in some facilities [ 2 , 5 , 19 ]. In turn, there is a significant correlation between burnout and job satisfaction, with healthcare workers who are experiencing low levels of job satisfaction reporting higher degrees of burnout and emotional exhaustion [ 20 ]. A 2020 ‘call to action’ regarding burnout amongst emergency physicians [ 21 ] demanded a focus on measuring and addressing job satisfaction and highlighted its significant contribution to burnout [ 20 ]. Our findings suggest the three pillars of job satisfaction, namely autonomy, competence and connection, are all threatened by the current working conditions found in many EDs. Protective factors include fractional ED appointments, diversity and flexibility of practice, continuous learning and upskilling and professional supervision or peer support. However, not all participants feel this is sufficient to preserve career longevity. There is a great deal that EDs can learn from a clinician''s experience in retrieval medicine, where the work feels more meaningful, less diluted by bureaucratic constraints and less encumbered by administrative pressures. Job satisfaction is higher; attrition is lower. I do think retrieval medicine is probably one of the best complements to emergency medicine because you get what is essentially the best of emergency medicine just without a lot of the down‐sides. 5. Limitations The qualitative nature of this study means the findings cannot be considered ‘generalisable’ to all EM physicians. However, no new insights were emerging by the final interviews, indicating thematic sufficiency. We interviewed physicians spanning a range of ages and genders who work in hospitals with differing characteristics and catchment areas. Despite this diversity, remarkably similar themes emerged throughout their responses. This group of physicians was chosen as, anecdotally, PHARM has higher levels of job satisfaction and less turnover than departmental EM, thus allowing us to develop a helpful insight into potential positive drivers of EM job satisfaction. While the purpose of the study was not to directly compare differing EM roles, the experiences of the same group of physicians in the different branches of the speciality have provided useful insight into what is important to this workgroup. 6. Conclusions The nature of EM work remains inherently satisfying but is influenced by the culture and current working conditions found in many EDs, particularly when these factors impact the standard of clinical care physicians feel they can provide. Our findings offer practical guidance on protective factors for job satisfaction and sustainability, including fractional appointments, diversity and flexibility of practice, continuous professional development and access to professional supervision or peer support. To preserve career longevity in EM it is not enough to simply acknowledge factors that drive job satisfaction; implementation of sustainable initiatives to address the identified issues is required. Attrition amongst the current staff specialist group, combined with the negative impact on future recruitment, will further affect staffing, workload and the ability to take leave. The resulting negative feedback loop may have a significant impact on the viability of the speciality in future years. Conflicts of Interest The authors declare no conflicts of interest. Acknowledgements The authors would like to thank the study participants who generously made time to share their experiences for this research. Open access publishing facilitated by University of Otago, as part of the Wiley ‐ University of Otago agreement via the Council of Australian University Librarians. Myers J. A., Aldington S., and Powell D. M. C., “A Qualitative Examination of Job Satisfaction in Emergency Medicine From the Perspective of Physicians Also Working in Prehospital and Retrieval Medicine,” Emergency Medicine Australasia 37, no. 4 (2025): e70121, 10.1111/1742-6723.70121. Data Availability Statement The data that support the findings of this study are available on request from the corresponding author. The data are not publicly available due to privacy or ethical restrictions. References 1. Smith J., Keating L., Flowerdew L., et al., “An Emergency Medicine Research Priority Setting Partnership to Establish the Top 10 Research Priorities in Emergency Medicine,” Emergency Medicine Journal 34 (2017): 454–456. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Dixon E., Murphy M., and Wynne R., “A Multidisciplinary, Cross‐Sectional Survey of Burnout and Wellbeing in Emergency Department Staff During COVID‐19,” Australasian Emergency Care 25 (2022): 247–252. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Holmes J. L., “Sustaining a Long Career in Emergency Medicine: Issues for Emergency Physicians of all Ages,” Emergency Medicine Australasia 31, no. 6 (2019): 1112–1114. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Johns G., Waddington L., and Samuel V., “Prevalence and Predictors of Mental Health Outcomes in UK Doctors and Final Year Medical Students During the COVID‐19 Pandemic,” Journal of Affective Disorders 311 (2022): 267–275. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Nguyen J., Liu A., McKenney M., Liu H., Ang D., and Elkbuli A., “Impacts and Challenges of the COVID‐19 Pandemic on Emergency Medicine Physicians in the United States,” American Journal of Emergency Medicine 48 (2021): 38–47. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Nicholls M., Hamilton S., Jones P., et al., “Workplace Wellbeing in Emergency Departments in Aotearoa New Zealand 2020,” New Zealand Medical Journal 134, no. 1541 (2021): 96–110. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Chapman J., Barrett M., Thompson M., Stehman C., Hansen M., and Wegman M., “A National Survey of Job Satisfaction and Workload Among Emergency Medicine (EM) Residency Faculty,” Cureus 15, no. 2 (2023): e34982. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Darbyshire D., Brewster L., Isba R., Body R., Basit U., and Goodwin D., “Retention of Doctors in Emergency Medicine: A Scoping Review of the Academic Literature,” Emergency Medicine Journal 38, no. 9 (2021): 663–672. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Petravić L., Bajec B., Burger E., Tiefengraber E., Slavec A., and Strnad M., “Emergency Physician Personnel Crisis: A Survey on Attitudes of New Generations in Slovenia,” BMC Emergency Medicine 24, no. 1 (2024): 25. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Swancott L., Armstrong N., Roland D., Walters H. L., and Kirk K., “Emergency Department Workforces'' Experiences and Perceptions of Well‐Being From an International Perspective: A Scoping Review,” BMJ Open 14, no. 7 (2024): e087485. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Fitzgerald K., Yates P., Benger J., and Harris A., “The Psychological Health and Well‐Being of Emergency Medicine Consultants in the UK,” Emergency Medicine Journal 34, no. 7 (2017): 430–435. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. James F. and Gerrard F., “Emergency Medicine: What Keeps Me, What Might Lose Me? A Narrative Study of Consultant Views in Wales,” Emergency Medicine Journal 34, no. 7 (2017): 436–440. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Australasian College for Emergency Medicine , Pre‐Hospital and Retrieval Medicine [Internet]. (ACEM, 2021), https://acem.org.au/ . [ Google Scholar ] 14. Vears D. F. and Gillam L., “Inductive Content Analysis: A Guide for Beginning Qualitative Researchers,” Focus on Health Professional Education: A Multi‐Professional Journal 23, no. 1 (2022): 111–127. [ Google Scholar ] 15. Thornton V. and Hazell W., “Junior Doctor Strike Model of Care: Reduced Access Block and Predominant Fellow of the Australasian College for Emergency Medicine Staffing Improve Emergency Department Performance,” Emergency Medicine Australasia 20, no. 5 (2008): 425–430. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Khan M. S., Elahi N. S., and Abid G., “Workplace Incivility and Job Satisfaction: Mediation of Subjective Well‐Being and Moderation of Forgiveness Climate in Health Care Sector,” European Journal of Investigation in Health, Psychology and Education 11, no. 4 (2021): 1107–1119. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Sancton K., Sloss L., Berkowitz J., Strydom N., and McCracken R., “Low‐Acuity Presentations to the Emergency Department: Reasons for and Access to Other Health Care Providers Before Presentation,” Canadian